# SAIPEN Protocol (RFC)

## Part 1: CORE (Continuation Protocol)

### 1.1 Normative Rules
The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119.

- Agent MUST read `STATE.md`, `BOARD.md`, and tail of `LOG.md` before execution. All three, and `KNOWLEDGE/`/`kitchen/`, live under `.saipen/` at the project root -- later mentions in this document may drop the prefix for brevity, the location never changes. **Unqualified means exactly `.saipen/STATE.md` (and siblings) at the project root, never a nested one.** Since v7.35.0, `.saipen/extensions/subs/<name>/STATE.md` and (during a parallel TRANSLATE run) `.saipen/saitranslate/STATE.md` are real files with the same names, nested deeper in the same tree -- each is that *other* instance's own state, distinguished by its full path, never substituted for or confused with the project's own. Do not `find`/glob for a bare filename and act on whatever turns up first; use the exact path. **No `.saipen/` at the project root and git is available?** Before concluding "not initialized" and defaulting to `saipen set` (which would create a second, disconnected `.saipen/` -- orphaning the real one): check whether the current directory is a *linked* git worktree, not the main one -- `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` returns a bare `.git` when it is the main worktree, and an actual path ending in `/.git` when it is a linked one (e.g. `/path/to/main-repo/.git`). `.saipen/` is gitignored by design, and git populates a linked worktree from tracked content only -- so it never arrives there, however healthy the main worktree is (`tests/scenarios/linked-worktree-not-uninitialized/`). Strip the trailing `/.git` to get the main worktree's root and look there; only genuinely absent at *that* path does "not initialized, run `saipen set`" hold.
- **Bind the project root before the first checkpoint read, and keep that binding for every read and write in the session.** An explicit project-root argument wins deliberately; otherwise a Git checkout resolves through `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` plus `--git-common-dir`, and a non-Git project resolves to the nearest ancestor already carrying `.saipen/`. The active worktree is asked first: a linked worktree carrying its own `.saipen/` IS its own project root, because a validator that reports on a different tree than the agent edited is green about work nobody did — reproduced as a linked worktree whose local `STATE.md` said `phase: NOT-A-PHASE` and validated clean against the main repository. Only a linked worktree with no `.saipen/` of its own — the normal case, the folder being gitignored — falls back to the main worktree's shared `.saipen/`; it MUST NOT create a local second copy to change that. Root resolution identifies the directory only; missing or corrupt `STATE.md` is then diagnosed by normal validation/Recovery, never misreported as an unowned cwd. After binding, unqualified checkpoint names are shorthand for absolute paths under that one root -- changing cwd never retargets them. Before a write from a changed cwd, resolve again: a different repository/common Git directory is a mismatch and the agent MUST refuse the relative write, return to the bound root, or explicitly bind a new target. **`saipen_home` is the protocol installation, not the project root; STATE deliberately stores no absolute project path because moving or cloning a valid project must not make its memory corrupt.** `tools/validate.py --project-root PATH` is the explicit form and may be invoked from another cwd; without it, a cwd with no owning memory FAILs instead of guessing or creating `.saipen/`. The SAIPEN home is located by the protocol install, never by scanning the workspace, its parents, or its siblings — it is commonly a sibling of the workspace — and a `.saipen/` found anywhere but `<project_root>/.saipen/` is another project's memory.

    **`protocol_dir` is the subdirectory of `saipen_home` that holds the actual protocol documents.** Two layouts exist and the resolution is deterministic:
    - Source clone (git repo): `<saipen_home>/saipen/BOOT.md` exists → `protocol_dir = <saipen_home>/saipen`.
    - Flattened install (skill injection): `<saipen_home>/saipen/BOOT.md` does NOT exist but `<saipen_home>/BOOT.md` does → `protocol_dir = <saipen_home>`.
    - Neither: `saipen_home` is dead and `BOOT.md` step 2 sets `BLOCKED` before any phase can be entered.

    Every normative document — `BOOT.md`, `STYLE.md`, `INDEX.md`, `CORE.md`, `MAINTENANCE.md`, `RFC.md`, `UI.md`, `HABITS.md`, and every `phases/*.md` — loads from `protocol_dir`. Runtime tools (`tools/`), schemas (`extensions/schemas/`), templates (`extensions/templates/`), and `VERSION` resolve from `saipen_home` directly. This is the one definition; phase docs, adapters, and `SKILL.md` that resolve a protocol path through any other derivation are in violation.
- **If any document disagrees, CORE.md wins.** `BOOT.md`, `phases/*.md`, `STYLE.md`, `CONFORMANCE.md`, `state.schema.json` and `KNOWLEDGE/*.md` all change across versions and can disagree, with each other or with a stale copy of this one. `CORE.md` resolves it, always -- not even `BOOT.md`'s "execute, don't ask" overrides a normative rule here, being a loading-speed optimization. Phase docs may tighten a rule for their own scope (verify.md's 3-hypothesis cap on top of § 1.6's table); they may never relax one. `extensions/schemas/state.schema.json` is the machine-readable mirror of STATE's field set and types -- authoritative for what a *machine* checks (`tools/validate.py` reads it directly), and the place to look for a field's type or enum. It is not a second opinion about what is required: **§ 1.2's nine-field required set is the normative list**, and the schema's own `required` array is deliberately narrower, because a JSON Schema `required` array cannot express "except on a fresh INIT" or "only when `execution_intent: goal`". A field absent from that array is therefore either genuinely optional or conditionally required and checked in `tools/validate.py` instead -- the schema's `description` says which, every time. Read the two together, never the array alone. The schema's descriptions are summaries of § 1.2's semantics, never overrides. This paragraph itself is a rule, not a comment: document hierarchy is load-bearing for every validation and every agent session, and an agent that encounters a conflict and resolves it in favor of the non-RFC document is in violation.
- **A new section names the defect class it eliminates, or it does not get written.** This governs every shipped document -- this one, `BOOT.md`, `phases/*.md`, `CONFORMANCE.md`, `KNOWLEDGE/*.md` -- and it is one question with a yes or no answer: *what can a conformant agent do today that this text makes non-conformant tomorrow?* No answer means no section. Prose that eliminates no defect costs every agent that reads it, forever, and implies coverage that does not exist -- which is worse than silence, because the next reader stops looking for the check that was never there. Three shapes fail the question and all three are common: a restatement of a rule that already exists somewhere else (cite it instead; two copies drift, and this document's own history is mostly that), an explanation of why a rule is good rather than what it forbids, and a requirement made of a phrasing nobody reads -- fixing an exact sentence when the machine matches a token, which happened here and lasted one release before it had to be narrowed. **No byte cap comes with this**, and the reason is worth one line so nobody adds one later: `BOARD.md` has a soft cap because it is read in full at every cold start, and none of these files are -- `BOOT.md` is the cold-start read, this document is reached only when a rule question arises, and `CONFORMANCE.md` is never read by a working agent at all. A cap without that cost behind it would be a number somebody has to argue with, not a defect prevented.
- Agent MUST NOT rely on chat context for project state.
- **A returning agent MUST distrust its own memory before acting on it.** The rule above is usually read as advice for a *cold* agent, which is the harmless case -- it has no memory to mislead it. The dangerous case is the warm one: an agent that worked this project earlier, remembers plausible specifics, and is wrong because somebody else moved the project in between. Its recollection looks like knowledge, so nothing prompts a re-read. This is mechanical to catch and costs one comparison: before acting on anything you believe you already know, check `STATE.md`'s `agent:` and `updated:`. **`agent:` is not you, or `updated:` is newer than your own last write -> your memory is stale by definition.** Re-read `STATE`/`BOARD`/the `LOG` tail from disk and treat every prior belief -- versions, ticket states, what you think you shipped -- as unverified until the file says so. (`tests/scenarios/returning-agent-stale-memory/` records the incident.) Confident staleness is worse than ignorance, because ignorance asks.
- Agent MUST degrade capabilities if host tools are missing.
- Agent MUST NOT write secrets (API keys, tokens, passwords, private credentials) into `STATE.md`, `BOARD.md`, `LOG.md`, `KNOWLEDGE/`, `.saipen/kitchen/`, `.saipen/extensions/`, `.saipen/saitranslate/kitchen/`, `.saipen/recovery/`, or `.saipen/logs/` -- every one of these is scratch or committed content meant to be read by other agents, kitchen files included. `.saipen/recovery/` and `.saipen/logs/` matter most, because nothing writes them deliberately: Recovery copies a corrupt `STATE.md` **verbatim** (§ 1.5) and sealing moves LOG lines verbatim (§ 1.2), so a secret reaching either original is faithfully archived by a mechanism whose job is not to alter content. Redact at discovery, before it can be archived: a Recovery copy of a state containing a secret MUST be redacted in the copy (the redaction is the one content change Recovery is allowed to make, and it MUST be noted in the accompanying LOG line), and a secret found in an already-sealed segment is a rotate-the-credential incident first -- append-only forbids quietly editing the segment, so redact it only as a deliberate, LOGged `DEC` repair, never as a silent rewrite. A secret discovered mid-task gets redacted in whatever gets written (`sk-***`, not the real value) and the user gets told to rotate it, same as any other finding. This covers any credential shape -- `ghp_`, `AKIA`, a `postgres://user:pass@host` string, a bearer token, anything that would let someone else authenticate as this project -- never just the one prefix easiest to name.
- Agent MUST NOT perform a destructive operation without explicit user confirmation, unless the active ticket itself pre-authorizes it AND the operation is reversible. Destructive includes at minimum: force-push, branch deletion, history rewrite, schema/database drop, mass file deletion, user data deletion, and any irreversible migration. This governs SAIPEN's own operation (SHIP, CLEAN, and any phase that touches disk or git) -- it does not relax or replace whatever confirmation discipline the operating agent's own platform already enforces, it is the floor every SAIPEN-conformant agent MUST meet regardless of vendor.
- **Reply Language**: `STYLE.md` declares `reply_language:`, and that ONE line decides. Its closed set is `et` (the shipped default: Estonian, always, whatever language the message arrived in), `en`, `ru`, and `auto`. At `et`/`en`/`ru` the value is the whole rule -- no detection, no tie-breaker, no precedence to reason about, and an agent that "corrects" the setting by answering in the user's language instead is in violation. `auto` selects the precedence rule that preceded this setting, preserved verbatim because it is genuinely useful and reconstructing it from memory is how it drifted twice before. A value outside the closed set is a validation FAILURE, never a silent fallback: guessing what `reply_language: eesti` meant is exactly the ambiguity the setting removes. The setting governs chat only -- protocol, code, commits, `KNOWLEDGE/` and `CHANGELOG.md` stay English at every value. Under `auto`: Reply-language precedence: explicit current user prose (Estonian/English/Russian) > clearly Russian primary repository for bare/ambiguous input > Estonian default; another detected language uses English. The current substantive request controls; if it genuinely switches language, its last substantive clause wins. Quoted material, code, paths, pasted logs, translated locale trees, OS/IDE locale, and platform UI MUST NOT count as user-language evidence. Repository language is a tie-breaker only when user prose is absent or ambiguous: Russian requires a clearly Russian root README and ordinary first-party project docs, not one file or locale, and MUST NOT override explicit Estonian or English prose.
- **Chat Voice & Compression**: The agent MUST apply `saipen/STYLE.md` (`caveman-дед` voice: structural compression, cut filler/articles/pleasantries/hedging, blunt/street-smart tone, zero preambles/postambles, zero corporate apologies) to EVERY response from the first token. Voice persistence: caveman-дед applies to every response until explicit "stop caveman" or "normal mode". `BOOT.md` MUST order the `STYLE.md` read in its numbered fast path, before any output, with a self-locating reference (the file in the same folder as `BOOT.md` itself, never a bare `<saipen_home>` pointer that needs resolution), and MUST NOT defer it solely to a rule-question escalation.

### 1.2 File Model
- **STATE.md**: MUST contain frontmatter: `phase`, `task`, `next_action`, `blocker`, `agent`, `saipen_version`, `mode`, `updated`, `transition_from`. **These nine are THE required set -- the single place it is written down.** § 1.5's checkpoint self-check, `BOOT.md`'s post-checkpoint re-read, and `CONFORMANCE.md` row 44 all point here instead of restating it, -- five copies of this list once drifted into five different answers, so `tools/validate.py` now FAILs any document that re-enumerates it (CONFORMANCE 58). Anything not on this nine-field list is not required, however emphatically another document phrases it. Four conditional additions, and no others: `transition_from` is omitted **only** on a fresh `INIT` (no previous phase exists to name); when `execution_intent: goal` the counters `goal_waves` and `goal_tickets` are additionally REQUIRED and MUST be integers (MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4); a Core `STATE.md` at `schema_version: 3` MUST carry `last_event` whenever its LOG contains an `E-###` event; and that same current-schema state MUST carry `style_contract`, the voice marker below. `schema_version` is deliberately NOT on the list -- it SHOULD be present and current, but absence or any revision below the current one is readable legacy, not corruption: it WARNs and MUST upgrade **to the current schema version** at the next checkpoint, which § 1.5's checkpoint rule names and `tools/validate.py` reads from `state.schema.json`'s `x-current-schema-version`. **This sentence names no number on purpose.** It named `v2` for as long as the schema had moved to 3, so the one instruction for escaping legacy state ordered a value the validator immediately WARNs as legacy -- a rule that hardcodes a version the thing it describes outgrows, and one nothing could see because the number read as fact. `saipen_version` (integer) records which protocol version wrote this state, and doubles as the version guard below -- one field. It is the MAJOR component only of the SAIPEN home's own `VERSION` file (semver `X.Y.Z` -- `saipen_version` is `X`, e.g. `7` for `7.46.0`, never the full string). "Current" for the version guard below means whatever `X` this agent's own `<saipen_home>/VERSION` currently reads -- CORE.md itself intentionally carries no version number of its own; the `VERSION` file at the saipen_home root (one directory above CORE.md) is the single source of truth for that. `saipen_home` (string) is a recognized MAY field: § 1.7's bootloader pointer, the absolute path to the SAIPEN home on the machine that last checkpointed -- MAY be absent on states written before v7.25.0. `next_action` MUST be an immediately executable command -- this includes the exact form `WAIT: <the specific question or decision needed>`, which counts as executable because "ask this exact thing and stop" is itself an unambiguous action. `next_action` MAY carry a compact trailing progress tag -- a single bracketed `[...]` suffix at the very end, informational only, never altering the command in front of it (e.g. `PHASE SCOUT T-154 [3/7 done, 1 blocked]`). An example that fails the rule below is worse than no example: a weak model copies the example and never reaches the rule. **`WAIT:` carries a category token, and the set of tokens is closed.** The shape is `WAIT: <category> -- <the exact question>`, where `<category>` is one of exactly seven words: `manual-verify`, `destructive-op`, `first-publish`, `user brake`, `blocked`, `safety valve`, `init`. `tools/validate.py` FAILs any `WAIT:` that does not open with one of them. This exists because § 1.11 names the guessing agent as the one failure this protocol cannot detect after the fact -- and the stopping agent is its twin: a vague `WAIT: need more context` is byte-identical in shape to a legitimate gate, passes every prefix check while saying nothing, and parks the project on a question nobody can answer. Naming the category is what makes the two mechanically separable, and it costs one word. The category is not decoration: it tells the human *what kind of answer unblocks this*, which is the difference between a `WAIT:` someone can clear in ten seconds and one that sits for a week. **And the body is exactly one sentence.** The category bounds what KIND of stop this is; nothing bounded what follows it, so `next_action` became a scratchpad -- and a stop instruction carrying notes is a stop instruction the next agent reads as a queue. This repository's own live state proved it: a `user brake` whose body ran three sentences of handoff status and finished by naming a ticket as still open "for a future run", after which the next agent went and scouted that ticket instead of honouring the brake. `tools/validate.py` FAILs a `WAIT:` body that begins a second sentence. The session report belongs in `.saipen/kitchen/digest.md`, which § 1.10's `saipen stop` writes for exactly that purpose, and queued work belongs on `BOARD.md`, where the Pick Rule can see it and this field cannot smuggle it past § 1.11's priority. The seven, in full -- `WAIT:` is legal ONLY for a manual-verify gate, a destructive-operation confirmation, a first-publish confirmation, the user's own explicit brake, a session-level `BLOCKED` unblock request (`phases/blocked.md`: a specific credential, decision, or manual intervention needed to resume -- the question itself must be concrete, never "what's blocking us?"), a tripped MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4 safety valve (the run hit its 3-wave/20-ticket ceiling and MUST stop until the user re-authorizes: `WAIT: safety valve reached (N waves / M tickets) -- run 'cc' to continue`; without this category the one place MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4 explicitly orders a stop-and-wait would have no legal way to express it), or -- narrowly -- `INIT` bootstrap when `BOARD.md` is empty and no project goal exists anywhere yet (`phases/init.md`): that bootstrap `WAIT:` MUST ask for the first goal or raw backlog only, never for context `STATE.md`/`BOARD.md`/`LOG.md` would otherwise already provide. Never a stand-in for "figure out project context," which the agent is already required to get from `STATE.md`/`BOARD.md`/`LOG.md`, not the user. **Narrower still at `phase: DONE` with an empty `## TODO`**: only three categories survive there, because every other one names a question about work in flight and there is none. All three MUST be written in a fixed, machine-recognizable form, since an agent arriving cold has no way to tell a legitimate pause from a previous agent's drift by reading free prose:
  - the MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4 safety valve -- `WAIT: safety valve reached (N waves / M tickets) -- run 'cc' to continue`. **The resume key is uniform** (T-###): `cc` (continue / bare `saipen`) reauthorizes a tripped valve and resumes the run for BOTH `execution_intent: goal` (resetting both counters and continuing the same objective) and `execution_intent: converge`. `saipen goal` is never a resume key -- it is the create/pivot command, so a pause naming it would substitute the objective instead of continuing it
  - the user's own explicit brake -- `WAIT: user brake -- <what the user asked to hold for>`
  - untriaged MARKHUNT findings -- `WAIT: blocked -- untriaged MARKHUNT findings in ## BLOCKED; triage into ## TODO or dismiss`. `phases/markhunt.md` records findings that are unvetted by construction, and `phases/done.md` holds the auto-proceed until a human has sorted them; without a fixed form here that brake had no legal way to exist at all. Both phase docs stated it, `tools/validate.py` already carved it out of the deadlock check, and this list said two -- so an agent obeying its own phase doc wrote a state this section called non-conformant, while the tool it would be judged by quietly agreed with the doc. The brake is real; what it lacked was a wording.

  **This restricts `WAIT:` only.** A `WAIT:` in that exact state that matches neither fixed form is non-conformant, `tools/validate.py` FAILs it, and § 1.11's UNBLOCK exception says to auto-transition instead of stopping. The other four `next_action` prefixes (`saipen `, `PHASE `, `RUN:`, `RESUME:`) are governed by their own rules and are **not** restricted here -- read the other way round, this paragraph would forbid the `PHASE HUNT` that MAINTENANCE.md § 2.1 requires from precisely this state, i.e. it would ban the only conformant exit. The fixed wording is the whole mechanism: a brake nobody can distinguish from drift gets treated as drift. `mode` is written per § 1.3 capability negotiation. `task` MAY be the literal string `none`. `blocker` MUST be non-empty when `phase: BLOCKED` -- a blocked state with no stated reason is not conformant. `updated` MUST be ISO-8601 **UTC** specifically (`Z` suffix or `+00:00`, never a local offset) -- Recovery's "STATE stale vs LOG/BOARD" comparison (§ 1.5) silently miscompares across agents in different timezones otherwise. MAY contain `execution_intent` (default `normal`) — the ONE canonical persisted execution-intent enum, closed to `normal | goal | converge` (MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4). While `execution_intent: goal`, MUST also carry `goal_waves` and `goal_tickets` (integers, both start at `0`) — the persisted safety-valve counters MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4 defines; absent/cleared once the intent leaves `goal`. The legacy `goal_mode: true|false` boolean remains READ-compatible only during migration — it maps deterministically (`true` -> `goal`, `false` -> `normal`) and MUST be dropped at the next checkpoint; a state carrying both fields is corrupt and FAILs validation (MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4). MAY contain `human_note` (string) — an optional one-line human→agent nudge ("last time you did X, don't"), read on the next continue (`BOOT.md`'s `human_note` step) and cleared once applied. It is a lightweight feedback channel, NOT a ticket or a goal: it never queues work, it just colors how the agent approaches the next action, then it's gone. A `human_note` that actually implies work still gets turned into a real `TODO` ticket, not left living in `STATE.md`. "Cleared once applied" MUST leave a trace, not just an empty field where text used to be -- LOG one line (`DEC: applied human_note: <the text>`, or `DEC: human_note -> T-###` when it became a ticket) in the same checkpoint that clears it, so a later Recovery or a hum…7916 tokens truncated…it instruction for "similar additions" — inferring and creating tickets for obvious missing workflow steps, complementary features, or logical extensions required to complete the pattern (aligning with § 2.3).
- **STATE attempt pointer**: `attempt: A-###` MAY exist -- the one optional
  pointer naming the OPEN Attempt episode currently executing the claimed
  Work in `task:`. An **Attempt** is one bounded execution episode of one
  agent on one ticket (T-1148): it is NOT a ticket, never appears on
  `BOARD.md`, and its failure MUST NOT touch the Work's identity,
  description or history. The pointer is written and cleared only by the
  engine's attempt open/close operations inside the OPS transaction layer --
  never by hand -- and is legal exactly when `task:` names a real claimed
  ticket and an unclosed machine-owned Attempt event for that id and ticket
  exists in `LOG.md`; any other combination is torn state that
  `tools/validate.py` FAILs. Absence is normal: projects with no attempt
  events simply have no recorded episodes, and none may be invented for
  them.
- **CCC routing discriminator**: `converge_target: done | ship | crew` MAY exist only
  while `execution_intent: converge`. Absent/`done` is plain `cc`; `ship` is
  `ccc` and persists the I -> SHIP -> J boundary across crashes. Clear it when
  convergence clears. `CONVERGE.md` owns both routes.
- **Trailing Extrapolation ('etc.')**: If a user prompt, backlog list, or feature request contains "etc.", "etcetera", "and so on", "и т.д.", or similar phrasing, the agent MUST interpret it as an explicit instruction for "similar additions" — inferring and creating tickets for obvious missing workflow steps, complementary features, or logical extensions required to complete the pattern (aligning with MAINTENANCE.md § 2.3).
- **Version guard**: If a project's `saipen_version` is HIGHER than what this agent's own copy of CORE.md defines as current, a newer protocol wrote this state and this agent is running a stale copy -- it MUST NOT silently proceed or rewrite the value downward. Degrade to `mode: read-only` and report the mismatch, or `BLOCKED` if even reading isn't safe, until the RFC copy is refreshed. Symmetric failure at the other end: if `<saipen_home>/VERSION` is missing, unreadable, or holds no parseable semver major, the guard's "current" is undefined and cannot be evaluated at all -- the agent MUST NOT silently proceed or write `STATE.md` on a guessed value (that write would stamp a `saipen_version` nothing verified). Degrade to `mode: read-only`, report the missing/unreadable `VERSION`, and refuse to downgrade or invent `saipen_version` until it is restored.
- **Executable `next_action`**: `next_action` MUST begin with one of `WAIT:`, `saipen `, `PHASE `, `RUN:`, or `RESUME:`. **Each prefix has exactly one argument shape, and a cold agent MUST be able to act on it without interpreting anything**  -- a prefix that passes every shape check while saying nothing about what to do is not a command:
  - `WAIT: <the exact question or authorization needed>` -- output that exact question verbatim and stop. Legal only in the categories enumerated below.
  - `saipen <command>` -- a command § 1.10 defines, or one an active project extension defines (§ 1.9). Route it there. A verb matching neither is non-conformant even though the shape is legal (`CONFORMANCE.md` row 49): `saipen hunt` looks perfect and names a phase only § 2.1 may enter autonomously.
  - `PHASE <phase-enum> [T-###]` -- enter that phase (a value from § 1.6's phase enum, uppercase) for that ticket, loading `<saipen_home>/phases/<lowercase-phase>.md` for its rules. **The ticket ref is REQUIRED for exactly the five phases this section already names as ticket-bearing** -- `SCOUT`, `BUILD`, `VERIFY`, `REVIEW`, `SHIP` -- and omitted for every other phase. A complement, not a second list: a hand-kept list here drifts from the one above the moment either changes, and its v7.92.0 version silently excluded `INIT`/`ADD`/`TRANSLATE`/`PREPARE`, leaving § 2.1's mandatory `HUNT -> ADD` with no legal `next_action` to write.
  - `RUN: <the exact shell, validator, or test command>` -- execute it as written and record the result in `LOG.md` as a `RUN:` line. Not a description of a command; the literal command.
  - `RESUME: T-### <phase-enum>` -- re-enter that phase on that specific ticket, picking up work already in flight (check `kitchen/` first, § 1.2). Distinct from `PHASE` only in intent, identical in obligation.
  Any of the five MAY carry the trailing `[...]` progress tag described above; nothing else may follow the argument. Vague continuation phrases are non-conformant, including `continue work`, `proceed`, `do next`, `review stuff`, `keep going`, `maybe`, and `if needed`. A question mark outside `WAIT:` is also non-conformant: questions belong in the explicit wait gate, not in a fake command. When `phase` is `SCOUT`, `BUILD`, `VERIFY`, `REVIEW`, or `SHIP`, `task` SHOULD reference a concrete `T-###`; `task: none` in those phases is allowed only for an explicitly named ticket-less maintenance exception in `next_action`.
- **STATE freshness marker**: Core `last_event: N` stores the numeric `N` from the highest `E-N` in the complete LOG sequence at the checkpoint that produced `STATE.md`. It is REQUIRED when `schema_version: 3` and at least one event exists; a fresh bootstrap with an empty LOG omits it because there is nothing truthful to name. Absence or any `schema_version` below the current one is readable legacy and WARNs rather than trapping an existing project, but the next checkpoint MUST write the current schema plus the marker. A present marker is always enforced, including in legacy state: lower than the LOG tail means stale, higher means corrupt or from an incompatible branch. Recovery upgrades legacy state and writes the actual tail; it never guesses a value from `updated:` or BOARD.

- **STATE voice marker**: Core `style_contract: <token>` stores the boot marker declared at the top of `STYLE.md`, and is `last_event`'s shape applied to the one boot duty no artifact could witness. § 1.1 has required reading `STYLE.md` before any output since v7.23.0, `BOOT.md` orders it as fast-path step 1, and a live session still read `BOOT.md`, this document and the phase docs while never opening it -- because a read leaves no trace, and a rule nothing can see is a rule only a strong model keeps. This does not prove the read; nothing can. It removes the silence: the duty now carries a value, and a value can be wrong out loud. REQUIRED when `schema_version: 3`; any lower or absent revision is readable legacy that WARNs and MUST write the marker at its next checkpoint. Always enforced when present -- a token that disagrees with `STYLE.md` means the checkpoint was written against a voice contract that is not the installed one. The token is derived from `STYLE.md`'s own text, so editing the contract invalidates every state that has not re-read it, and it is declared in `STYLE.md` alone: a value reachable from `BOOT.md` would be copyable without ever opening the contract it stands for, which is the exact failure being closed. `tools/validate.py` prints the current value whenever it disagrees, so no agent is ever stuck guessing it. Recovery writes it from `STYLE.md`, never from memory or from the state it is repairing.
- **BOARD.md**: MUST track ticket status via section headings `## DOING` / `## TODO` / `## DONE` / `## BLOCKED`. Every ticket line MUST follow this exact shape: `- [ ] T-### description`, checkbox `[ ]` open / `[x]` done / `[/]` in-progress. **The checkbox MUST agree with the section it sits under**: `[x]` only under `## DONE`, `[/]` only under `## DOING`, `[ ]` under `## TODO` or `## BLOCKED`. The section is the status; the checkbox is how a human skims it, and a board where the two disagree gives two different answers to "is this done" depending on which one the reader trusts. `tools/validate.py` FAILs a mismatch. Ticket lines MAY be optionally followed by any of ` | needs: T-###,T-###`, ` | owner: AgentID`, ` | claim_time: ISO8601 UTC` (same `Z`/`+00:00` requirement as `STATE.md`'s `updated`, § 1.4 -- the 15-minute staleness window in § 1.4 silently miscompares across agents in different timezones otherwise, identical failure mode to `updated`), ` | blocker: facts + dead ends`, ` | verify: <command or criterion>` (`phases/plan.md`'s "independently verifiable" ticket shape, made concrete on the ticket itself) as space-pipe-separated fields -- only the fields that apply, in any order. **Changing a ticket's status MUST move its line: cut from the old heading, paste under the new one. A ticket MUST NOT appear under two headings at once, and MUST NOT be left duplicated under its old section.** `T-###` IDs MUST be unique and MUST NOT be reused once assigned, even after the ticket is `DONE` or pruned. **The board alone cannot tell you what has been assigned**: `phases/clean.md` prunes `DONE` tickets, so the highest ID visible is routinely lower than the highest ever issued and "max on board + 1" silently reuses a pruned ID. The next ID is one above the highest canonical ticket ID: the `T-###` values
that actually appear as BOARD ticket lines (canonical) or as the structured
`[T-###]` event field of LOG lines -- never free-prose mentions of a `T-###`
inside a description or a log message body, which are not identity (the
T-991/T-992 prose-poisoning class). Walk back into sealed segments when the
active tail does not reach far enough (the same walk § 1.5 does for goal
counters). A literal `|` inside `description` MUST be escaped as `\|` --
unescaped, it is indistinguishable from a field separator. `## BLOCKED` holds ticket-level blocks only (`phases/verify.md`'s debug cap: facts + dead ends noted via the `| blocker:` field) — distinct from session-level `STATE.phase: BLOCKED` (§ 1.6, `phases/blocked.md`), which is reserved for when no ticket anywhere on the board is workable. The Pick Rule (§ 1.6) only ever selects from `## TODO`, so a `## BLOCKED` ticket is automatically excluded without extra filtering logic. **That is also where a ticket goes when its completion condition can never be met.** A ticket that owns a permanent warning is the worked case: immutable history guarantees the warning emits forever, the ticket exists so the slug has an owner, and its own `verify:` says closing it while the warning emits FAILs. In `## TODO` such a ticket is workable by every test the Pick Rule applies -- open checkbox, no unmet `needs:`, unclaimed -- so the board ORDERS an agent to take work nobody can finish, and two honest agents then diverge: one adopts it and produces nothing, the other skips it and breaks the rule that the topmost workable ticket wins. `## BLOCKED` with a `| blocker:` naming why the condition is permanent resolves it with no new mechanism: the section already excludes the ticket from the Pick Rule, § 2.1 already excludes it from the halt test so the board can still reach `HUNT`, and the ticket is still live enough to own its slug. It is not `## DONE` -- that is a closure claim, and nothing was closed. **The same section holds a ticket whose work another instance owns by rule.** `phases/translate.md` gives 29 of the 32 languages to a dedicated instance and tells a Core agent that finds them stale not to start grinding through them; a ticket for that work sitting in `## TODO` is therefore workable by every test the Pick Rule applies while the phase doc forbids Core to perform it -- the same divergence as above, arrived at from the other side, with one agent doing the forbidden work and the other skipping a ticket the rule says it must take. `| blocker:` names the owner and what clears it (the command that runs that instance), so the block is a real one somebody can lift rather than a shelf. **Keep it readable.** `BOARD.md` is read on every cold start, so an unbounded board is context a cold agent spends before doing any work. It is not append-only, so it needs no sealing -- `phases/clean.md`'s scrub is the whole mechanism, and pruned tickets lose nothing, their events living permanently in `LOG.md`/`CHANGELOG.md`. `tools/validate.py` WARNs past a ~16 KB soft cap; a `## DONE` section holding more prose than the active work is the usual cause, scrubbed at the next `CLEAN`. **Cyclic `needs:`** (CONFORMANCE.md requires the graph stay acyclic): on detection, the agent MUST move every ticket in the cycle to `## BLOCKED` with `| blocker: dependency cycle: T-###,T-###,...`, LOG a `DEC` line, and continue with other workable tickets -- never pick from inside a known cycle. **Dangling `needs:`** (a referenced `T-###` exists nowhere on the board) is worse than a cycle, not milder: a cycle is caught by the acyclic check, while a reference to a ticket that was never real leaves the Pick Rule permanently unsatisfiable with no signal at all. Same remedy: move the ticket to `## BLOCKED` with `| blocker: needs nonexistent T-###`, LOG a `DEC` line, keep working other tickets.
- **Board must reflect shipped work.** Before SHIP (§ 1.10), the agent MUST ensure every work unit done this session has a ticket in `## DONE`. Work performed inline without a ticket (maintenance fixes, mechanical sweeps, doc corrections) MUST get a new `## DONE` ticket before `saipen ship` proceeds. A board whose `## DONE` section omits visible or LOGged work is stale and blocks SHIP until corrected -- the board is the project's work surface, not an incomplete index of it.
  **That ticket still has to earn `## DONE`, and until v7.92.0 this rule read as permission to skip that.** § 1.6 is unconditional: a ticket MUST NOT be marked `DONE` without a successful `VERIFY`. Read together with the paragraph above, an agent under ship pressure could conclude the protocol was *ordering* it to manufacture `[x]` tickets for work nothing ever checked -- turning `## DONE` from a record of verified work into a receipt printer, which is exactly the "finished is not verified" confusion `saipen status` (§ 1.10) exists to expose. It never meant that. Inline work reaches `## DONE` only with a verify trace, meaning at least one of:
  - a `| verify: <command or criterion>` field on the ticket line whose criterion actually ran, or
  - a `LOG.md` `RUN:` or `H:` line proving the check passed, referenced by that ticket.

  For a doc, config, or maintenance sweep with no test of its own, `tools/validate.py PASS` is an acceptable minimum criterion -- low, but real, and it is the same check the pre-commit hook runs. No verify trace and none obtainable -> the ticket stays `## TODO` or moves to `## BLOCKED` with the reason, and SHIP stays blocked. Blocking the ship is the correct outcome here, not a problem to route around: the alternative ships unverified work under a green checkbox.
- **BOARD shape is validated as a whole, not just ticket lines.** The four section headings (`## DOING`, `## TODO`, `## DONE`, `## BLOCKED`) MUST each appear exactly once. Duplicate headings split the work surface and are non-conformant even when ticket IDs remain unique. `review_passes: N` is a recognized optional ticket field used by `phases/review.md` to enforce its two-pass cap mechanically instead of from memory.
- **The ticket-field list is closed, and this is it.** After the description, a ticket line MAY carry ` | field: value` pairs drawn only from: `needs:` (T-### dependencies), `owner:` and `claim_time:` (§ 1.4's claim pair -- both or neither), `blocker:` (why a `## BLOCKED` ticket is stuck, with evidence), `verify:` (the command or criterion that decides the ticket is done -- `phases/plan.md` asks for it while the goal is still fresh, rather than as an afterthought during VERIFY), `review_passes:` (above), `verify_attempts:` (an integer, absent reads as zero: the current failed-fix-cycle budget `phases/verify.md` spends on this ticket; `ticket unblock` clears that current budget while journaled block/unblock events retain historical evidence), and `source_reports:` (sweep provenance: the composite `IMP-###` identities a canonical ticket was generated from -- `saipen/IMPROVE.md` section 7's sweep-to-ticket link, resolved by the `[sweep-ticket-link]` check against `SWEEP.md`; a reference that resolves to no disposition, or a ticket produced by an unverified or `INVALID`/`ALREADY_FIXED` finding, FAILs), `recurrence:` (the META-IMPROVEMENT reasoning a protocol-level improve ticket MUST record -- the cross-project recurrence analysis; a protocol-level fix records whether the defect recurs across projects, a local bug stays local -- required by the `[sweep-ticket-link]` check for a `PROTOCOL_VIOLATION` finding that produced a ticket), and `weak_model:` (the WEAK-MODEL PRECEDENT answer -- could a weak but compliant model still choose wrong while honestly believing it followed SAIPEN? -- same requirement). **`blocker:` is active status, not history: it MUST be present and non-empty exactly under `## BLOCKED`, and MUST be absent under `## TODO`, `## DOING`, and `## DONE`. `ticket block` moves the line and sets the field atomically; `ticket unblock` moves it back, removes the field atomically, and MUST be given the decision/evidence that lifts the block -- an unblock with no stated reason refuses, because lifting an authorization boundary without naming the authorization is the same bypass as a silent raw edit.** Anything else is non-conformant and `tools/validate.py` FAILs it; a literal `|` inside a description MUST be escaped as `\|`. This list existed in the tool and in that FAIL message from the start, and `verify:` -- which 72 of this repository's own tickets carry -- was named nowhere in this document until v7.122.0, while `plan.md` and the validator both cited § 1.2 for it. **Every field on this list is optional except `blocker:` under `## BLOCKED` and `verify:` in `## DONE`; each is REQUIRED there and MUST be non-empty.** `## DONE` is a completion claim; what it claims is that the ticket's own `verify:` condition was met, so a ticket that arrives there without one asserts a result no reader can distinguish from an untested one. The rule is about presence, not grade: the field's content is evidence for a human or a reviewer to weigh, and a checker pretending to judge it would only add a third unfounded claim. A ticket closed WITHOUT being built is still legal -- superseded, withdrawn, decided away by the user -- and states exactly that in `verify:`, which is the difference between a board that says what happened and one that rounds every closure up to "verified".
- **LOG.md**: Append-only event graph. Every line MUST follow this exact
  shape, in this order: `- DATE [E-###] [parent: E-###] [T-### | T-none] [agent: <id>] TAXONOMY: text`.
  This skeleton line is a quick-reference shape, not a second source of
  truth -- `[E-###]` alone is MUST, every other bracketed slot here is MAY
  exactly as detailed in the bullets below; a conformant line may omit
  `parent:`, `agent:`, and even the ticket ref (bare `T-none`) entirely.
  - `DATE` MUST be human-readable `DD.MM.YY HH:mm` and MUST be UTC (not
    ISO-8601 -- ISO-8601 UTC lives only in `STATE.md updated`; `DD.MM.YY
    HH:mm` here is also UTC). Agent MUST use the real current UTC time,
    never a placeholder (`XX`, `TODO`, etc. are non-conformant).
    **The agent MUST read the clock rather than estimate it.** The number is
    not derivable from anything already in context -- not from the previous
    line, not from how long the work felt -- and an agent that reasons its
    way to a plausible minute has fabricated evidence in the one file
    Recovery reconstructs order from. Read it: `python -c "import
    datetime;print(datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).strftime('%d.%m.%y
    %H:%M'))"`. LOG timestamps SHOULD be monotonically non-decreasing within
    the active LOG and across sealed segments. A timestamp earlier than the
    previous event by more than 5 minutes is a WARN-level anomaly unless a
    later DEC line explicitly documents it as historical clock drift. A
    timestamp more than **5 minutes** ahead of current UTC is a FAIL-level
    anomaly -- the same slack the backwards check allows, because it stands
    for the same thing, clock skew between machines. The bound was 3 hours for most of
    this protocol's life and caught nothing real: agents do not miss the clock by
    hours, they miss it by twenty minutes, which is exactly what happened at
    E-1788..E-1793 with every line green.
    **An ahead-stamp is repaired, not waited out.** The rule said a future
    stamp FAILs and said nothing about clearing one, so the only precedent was
    to leave it and let real time catch up -- which E-1912 could afford at
    eleven minutes and E-2053 could not at 142, where it would have held every
    gate red for two and a half hours while asserting that work happened at a
    time it had not. Two honest agents diverge there, one waiting and one
    repairing, and the waiting one is defending a false record. So: restamp
    the line to a bound you can defend -- normally the parent event's minute,
    since the true time is unknowable after the fact -- and append a DEC
    naming the original value, the replacement, and that the minute is
    inherited rather than measured. Append-only forbids a SILENT rewrite, not
    a declared repair; this is the same treatment the U+FFFD clause below
    already prescribes, and inventing a plausible-looking minute instead is
    the fabrication the read-the-clock rule above exists to stop.
    U+FFFD replacement characters in LOG are a FAIL-level corruption signal:
    repair the shape/readability explicitly and add a new DEC line naming the
    repaired event; never hide the repair by silently rewriting history.
  - `[E-###]` (numeric Event ID) is MUST, always -- phase names
    (`[HUNT]`, `[BUILD]`) are NOT a substitute and MUST NOT appear here.
    IDs MUST be unique and MUST increase monotonically -- never reused,
    never assigned out of order.
  - `[parent: E-###]` is MAY (links the event graph; omit for a fresh root). When present, it MUST reference an `E-###` that already exists earlier in the file -- a dangling parent breaks the graph Recovery (§ 1.5) depends on.
  - `[T-###]` (ticket reference) is MAY -- omit for ticket-less events
    (HUNT sweeps, SHIP, session-level DEC/RUN), or write the literal
    `[T-none]` to mark ticket-lessness explicitly (equally legal -- it
    distinguishes "no ticket" from "forgot the field", and established
    practice already used it; ratified v7.24.0). Any other non-numeric
    value in this position is non-conformant.
  - `[agent: <id>]` (writer identity, same id convention as `STATE.md
    agent:`) is MAY, placed after the ticket ref and before the taxonomy.
    Single-agent sessions SHOULD omit it -- `STATE.md agent:` already says
    who's working, repeating it per line is noise.
  - `TAXONOMY` MUST be exactly one of: `RUN` (command executed, result),
    `DEC` (decision made, why), `H` (hypothesis, test, verdict). No other
    label is conformant -- a phase name is not a taxonomy value.
  - A LOG entry MUST record what already happened, never what is about to. The `text` portion's first clause -- up to its first ` -- `, ` -> `, `; ` or `. ` -- is where the line states what its event WAS, and it MUST NOT be written in the future tense or as an intention (`will`, `going to`, `about to`, `plans to`, `next step`, and the like). This is not a style preference: nothing downstream can tell an intention from a record. § 1.5's Recovery rebuild, an audit, and the next agent's cold start all read `RUN: will ship the release` exactly as they read `RUN: ship the release -> pushed abc1234` -- as evidence the act occurred -- so a line written ahead of its own work manufactures completion the moment it is appended, and append-only means it can never be taken back. Do the thing, then log it; a plan that needs recording is a `DEC` about a ticket, which is a fact ("decided X"), not a forecast. Later clauses may name a future that is not the writer's own claim -- a ticket's pending content, another agent's queue -- because the event the line reports has already been stated by then.
  - **A conformance run is recorded in a fixed, findable form.** When
    `tools/validate.py` (or the portable floor standing in for it, § 1.3) is
    run, the LOG line recording it MUST carry, as its `TAXONOMY: text`,
    exactly `RUN: validate.py -> PASS` or `RUN: validate.py -> FAIL`, and MAY
    continue after that with counts, context or commentary. § 1.10 orders
    `saipen status` to report "the result of the last `tools/validate.py` run
    if one is recorded in `LOG.md`" -- a duty with no shape behind it, so a
    cold agent either greps prose and guesses or truthfully answers "none
    recorded" while the LOG holds the answer. This is § 2.4's `DEC:
    goal_waves N->M` argument one field over: a record another rule is
    required to READ needs a form the reader can find, or the reading is
    invention. The result belongs to the run that produced it -- writing the
    line without running the command is the fabrication § 1.5's read-back
    discipline exists to stop.
  - **Attempt lifecycle events (T-1148)**: an Attempt episode opens and
    closes with machine-owned `DEC` lines the engine writes through OPS:
    `attempt A-### open` (optionally `; supersedes A-###` -- the previous
    episode on the same Work) and
    `attempt A-### close result <R> stop <S>` with optional bounded clauses
    ` -- evidence E-###,...` (at most eight, each an existing event) and
    `; unknown: <one line <=200 chars>` naming what remains genuinely
    unknown. The vocabularies are closed: result is one of
    `candidate | failed | interrupted | yielded | superseded` (`active` is
    derived from an open with no close and is never written); stop reason is
    one of `completed_execution | context_limit | provider_limit |
    provider_failure | process_crash | user_stop | capability_missing |
    validation_failure | deliberate_handoff | unknown`; and only the
    matrix-defined pairings are legal (e.g. `candidate` pairs only with
    `completed_execution`, `interrupted` never with `validation_failure`).
    IDs are unique forever, one episode open project-wide at a time
    (single-writer), every close names the same ticket as its open, and a
    successor that finds a crashed predecessor's dangling episode closes it
    as `interrupted` / stop `unknown` BEFORE claiming the Work. An agent
    that stops itself records the true reason (`context_limit`,
    `deliberate_handoff`, ...); unknown means exactly "the predecessor
    could not say", never "nothing happened".
  - Commentary voice (STYLE.md) wraps AROUND this exact skeleton; it never
    changes the skeleton's shape.
  - When a phase doc instructs "LOG exactly `RUN: X`" (or `DEC:`/`H:`) as a
    fixed format, that fixed text is the `TAXONOMY: text` portion only --
    the full skeleton above (`DATE`, `[E-###]`, etc.) always still applies.
    No phase doc's fixed-format instruction is ever license to write just
    the bare taxonomy:text fragment as the whole line.
  - **Segmentation (keep it small, CPU/RAM/disk-friendly).** Append-only
    does not mean one ever-growing file. A multi-thousand-line `LOG.md` is
    slow to read on weak hardware and can overflow a context window --
    breaking § 1.1's "read the tail of `LOG.md`" before it even starts.
    So `LOG.md` MAY be **segmented**: when it grows past a soft cap
    (~300 lines / ~64 KB), at the next checkpoint *seal* it -- move its
    current content verbatim into `.saipen/logs/LOG-<NNN>.md` (`NNN`
    zero-padded, ascending: `001`, `002`, ...) and start a fresh `LOG.md`
    that continues the very same `E-###` sequence. Sealing MOVES whole
    lines, it never rewrites them, so append-only holds across the segment
    boundary -- history is relocated, never edited. `E-###` stays globally
    unique and monotonic across every segment; `[parent: E-###]` resolves
    against the concatenation of all sealed segments (in `NNN` order)
    followed by the active `LOG.md`. Only the active `LOG.md` is ever read
    for normal operation -- § 1.1's tail read and § 1.5 Recovery both touch
    it alone and stay cheap no matter how long total history gets; sealed
    segments are cold storage, read only when a `parent:` chain or an audit
    actually walks back into them. `tools/validate.py` reads sealed
    segments + active `LOG.md` as one sequence, so the graph checks are
    unchanged by where the boundary falls. Sealing is a natural `CLEAN`
    step (`phases/clean.md`) but MAY happen at any checkpoint once the cap
    is crossed. **Sealing MUST be crash-safe**, same temp-file-plus-rename
    discipline § 1.5 already requires for `BOARD.md`/`STATE.md`: (1) write
    the sealed segment to `.saipen/logs/LOG-<NNN>.md` via temp file + rename
    first; (2) only once that succeeds, replace the active `LOG.md` with a
    fresh empty one, also via temp + rename; (3) never delete or truncate
    the original content before the sealed copy is confirmed on disk. A
    crash between steps 1 and 2 leaves the pre-seal `LOG.md` still intact
    and readable -- worst case the next agent re-attempts sealing on a file
    that's already safely duplicated, never a lost history. **Sealing MUST NOT begin
    unless the cap is actually crossed** -- that precondition is the outer
    guard, and the idempotency check below cannot substitute for it. The two
    protect against different things: after a *successful* seal the active
    log is a brand-new file, not a prefix of any segment, so a second run
    sails past the prefix test and seals a nearly-empty log into its own
    segment. Observed exactly once, in v7.93.0, from re-running a seal script
    to test its idempotency; the stray segment was folded back and the repair
    LOGged. Check the cap first, every time. **The re-attempt after a crash
    MUST also be idempotent, not a blind second seal**: before writing a new
    segment, compare the active `LOG.md`'s content against the
    highest-numbered existing `.saipen/logs/LOG-<NNN>.md`. Already identical
    (or a prefix of it) -> the seal already succeeded and only step 2 was
    lost: just replace the active `LOG.md` with a fresh empty one, do NOT
    create another segment. Skipping this check writes the same `E-###`
    lines into two segments, which breaks the global uniqueness the whole
    scheme rests on -- the exact wreckage sealing exists to avoid.
  - **A plain append is not automatically crash-safe either.** `LOG.md`
    grows by appending, which is cheap and normally atomic enough at these
    sizes -- but a crash mid-append can still leave a final line truncated
    mid-text. Such a line is debris, judged the same way § 1.2's kitchen
    rule judges a half-written scratch file: the *last* line of the active
    `LOG.md` failing to match the skeleton, when every line before it
    matches, is a crashed append, not history to preserve. Move it to
    `.saipen/recovery/<timestamp>-LOG-tail.md` and continue from the last
    intact line -- never guess at what the truncated line meant to say, and
    never treat it as license to reshape *earlier* lines (`phases/validate.md`'s
    shape-only rule still binds: a malformed line that isn't the last one
    is real history with a bad shape, a different problem entirely).
- **KNOWLEDGE/**: Directory for durable truths. MUST NOT contain event histories -- that is the actual constraint, not the filename. Two valid shapes, pick whichever fits the content: a living reference doc with a descriptive name (`architecture.md`, `traps.md`) that gets edited in place as understanding improves, or a numbered ADR (`ADR-001.md`, `ADR-002.md`, ...) recording one immutable decision + its reasoning, never edited once written. Either way: durable truth, never a log of events.
- **kitchen/**: Directory for intermediate, half-finished files, scratchpads, and work-in-progress data. Agents MUST store temporary work here to avoid cluttering the project root. If an agent terminates mid-task, the successor MUST inspect `kitchen/` to resume work seamlessly -- this means `.saipen/kitchen/` specifically, the Core project's own scratch. `TRANSLATE` (§ 2.1) keeps its own, separate `.saipen/saitranslate/kitchen/` — never shared with this one. Each subSaipen (`extensions/subs/`) likewise keeps its own `kitchen/` under `.saipen/extensions/subs/<name>/` — a crashed subSaipen's half-done work is that subSaipen's own concern to resume (or the main agent's, via the Handoff/OUTBOX procedure that extension defines), never something the main agent inspects looking for its own crashed work. **Kitchen writes are not required atomic** (unlike `STATE.md`/`BOARD.md`, § 1.5) -- so a kitchen file whose last record ends mid-line was truncated by a crash mid-write, and is debris, not resumable state: the successor MUST treat such a file as corrupt and restart that piece of scratch clean, never resume from a half-written kitchen file as if it were whole. The authoritative trio (`STATE`/`BOARD`/`LOG`) is protected by § 1.5's write-order; kitchen scratch trades that guarantee for cheapness, so integrity is checked on read, not assumed.

### 1.3 Capability Negotiation (Two-Way Handshake)
1. **Protocol Demands**: `STATE.md` MAY define `requires: [filesystem, git, shell, python]`.
2. **Agent Capabilities**: Agent MUST evaluate host against requirements.
3. **Mode Lock**: Agent MUST write operating mode to `STATE.md` (`mode: full | read-only | no-publish | manual-verify`). **`read-only` is the one mode that cannot record itself** -- it is set precisely because filesystem writes are unavailable, so "write `mode: read-only` to `STATE.md`" is unsatisfiable by construction. Resolve it the honest way, never by quietly skipping the capability check: report the detected mode in chat instead, work under it for the whole session, and leave whatever `mode:` the file already carries untouched (a stale `mode: full` on disk is not a lie the agent told -- it is simply the last value a writing agent left, and the next agent with write access re-negotiates it from its own host anyway). A later agent MUST NOT read a `mode:` value as proof of the *current* host's capabilities; § 1.3's handshake is re-run every session, this field records its last outcome, not a standing fact.
   - **A `requires:` entry with no mapping below** (e.g. `python`) is not a licence to ignore it: missing, and the work genuinely needs it -> degrade to the nearest mode that describes what's actually lost (no `python` means `tools/validate.py` can't run, so VALIDATE falls back to the frozen portable floor `tests/validate.sh`/`.ps1` per `phases/validate.md`, and only if *that* is also unavailable does the conformance check become impossible -> `BLOCKED` rather than a silent skip). Never proceed pretending an unavailable required tool was merely optional.
   - Missing Git: `mode: no-publish`. Agent MUST NOT perform any git-dependent step (commit, tag, push) -- but SHIP itself stays reachable: `phases/ship.md` defines a no-publish branch that still does everything that doesn't need git (version bump, CHANGELOG, digest) and closes straight to `DONE`. A blanket ban on entering `SHIP` at all would leave a git-less project unable to ever close a ticket (`phases/review.md` makes `SHIP` mandatory before `DONE`, no exception) -- degrading git-dependent steps only, same treatment as `read-only`/`manual-verify` degrade their own missing capability instead of dead-ending the whole phase (v7.66.0; a real, previously-unresolved gap here until then).
   - Missing Shell: `mode: manual-verify`.
   - Missing Filesystem Write: `mode: read-only`. **The rule is the principle, and the list is only its most common instances: every phase whose work product is a file write is out of reach.** **Read-only banned phases**: `INIT`, `PLAN`, `ADD`, `BUILD`, `SHIP`, `CLEAN`, `TRANSLATE`. The agent MUST NOT transition to any of them, nor make any `ADD`/`HUNT` change beyond reporting findings. `INIT` creates `.saipen/` and `PLAN` writes tickets, so both were always unreachable; naming only four made the list look exhaustive and left a read-only agent apparently free to stamp tickets it could not save. When a phase is not listed, apply the principle rather than assuming permission. `MARKHUNT`, `PREPARE`, and `VALIDATE` are the audit/validate phases that quietly also write, so read-only reaches them only in **report-only** form: run the audit / freshness-pack / conformance check and report the result in chat, but MUST NOT record it -- no `## BLOCKED` tickets from `MARKHUNT`, no `kitchen/` or handoff payload from `PREPARE`, no structural repair from `VALIDATE` (report the corruption, leave it as-is). The agent MAY still read, analyze, and report; it advises, it does not act. A **subSaipen**'s `mode: read-only` is a *scope* lock, not this capability lock: it writes its own folder freely and is barred only from the shared tree, so its phase ban is four phases rather than these seven -- `extensions/subs/PROTOCOL.md` § 1 states it, and the two lists are cross-checked. Claiming the two contracts were identical is what let a document forbid `PLAN` while every subSaipen legitimately planned its own backlog.
4. **Optional Parallel Execution**: `subagents` (parallel task/agent
   spawning) is never required and never gates a phase or sets `mode`.
   Its trigger is independence, not speed -- `HUNT`'s 6 signal categories
   (`phases/hunt.md`) don't depend on each other's results, so where
   subagents are available they MAY run concurrently instead of in turn.
   Absence MUST fall back silently to sequential execution -- same result.

### 1.4 Claim & Ownership
- Claiming a ticket = moving it to `## DOING` with `owner: <AgentID>` and `claim_time: <ISO8601 UTC>` set on its line. When one agent is provably alone on a project (the common single-agent case), the fields are OPTIONAL -- but any agent that even suspects it is not alone MUST set both on every ticket it picks.
- **Active claim**: `claim_time` less than 15 minutes old. The owner MUST refresh `claim_time` before it goes stale while holding the ticket: at every checkpoint (§ 1.5), AND as a standalone `BOARD.md`-only touch at least every 10 minutes of continuous mid-ticket work. Checkpoints alone cannot carry this duty -- § 1.5 checkpoints fire after tickets, so a 40-minute ticket would starve its own claim mid-work and make theft of a live ticket legal (v7.28.0 tied refresh to checkpoints only; fixed v7.30.0). Liveness is decided by reading `BOARD.md` alone; a recent `LOG.md` line stamped `[agent: <owner>]` (§ 1.2) MAY serve as supporting evidence, never as the primary test. (The pre-v7.28.0 "or actively writing to LOG.md" test was undecidable -- a shared file's mtime can't attribute activity to any particular owner).
- **Stale claim**: `claim_time` 15+ minutes old -> forfeit. The next agent MAY take the ticket over: LOG a `DEC` line recording the takeover (old owner, observed staleness), set its own `owner:`/`claim_time`, and inspect `kitchen/` for the predecessor's half-done work (§ 1.2) before redoing anything from scratch.
- A `## DOING` ticket carrying NO `owner:`/`claim_time` at all is unclaimed by definition (a crashed single-agent session, or pre-claim history): any agent MAY adopt it the same way -- LOG the `DEC`, check `kitchen/`, continue the work.
- **Conflicting claim writes**: the first write that lands in `BOARD.md` wins -- but whole-file saves mean a concurrent second save can silently clobber the first, so "landing" MUST be verified, never assumed: after writing a claim, re-read `BOARD.md` and confirm your claim actually survived. Your claim is absent or overwritten -> you lost, whatever the wall-clock order was; re-read and pick a different ticket -- never re-assert a claim over the winner's.
- **`agent:` names the SEAT, not the model build -- and a returning agent inherits it.** Every rule in this section compares `agent:` against "itself", and until v7.125.0 nothing said where an agent gets its own value, so each session invented one: this repository's own history carries six for what were two or three actors (`claude-opus`, `claude-sonnet-5`, `opencode`, `antigravity`, `antigravity-gemini`, `gemini-pro`), and one LOG line reached disk reading `[agent: id]` -- a placeholder, not a name. A comparison with one side undefined is not a safety mechanism; it is noise that fires both ways. **False alarm**: a model upgrade renames the seat, the next session sees a stranger and assumes a live concurrent agent. **Missed alarm**: two genuinely different actors both writing the same generic value are indistinguishable. So: **read `STATE.md`'s existing `agent:` and keep it** -- that value is the seat, and continuing another session's work in the same seat is what this protocol is for. Change it only when you are genuinely a different actor (a different person's tooling, a different platform driving the project, a subSaipen writing its own state), and when you do, LOG a `DEC` naming the old value and the new one so the graph shows a handover rather than an unexplained stranger. A model version change is NOT a different actor. **And the first value is DERIVED, not chosen: the seat is the agent home this protocol was loaded from.** `BOOT.md` already resolves that path to find the phase docs, so the value costs no new lookup: take the home directory's own name, strip a leading dot, lowercase it -- `.claude` -> `claude`, `.codex` -> `codex`, `.config/opencode` -> `opencode`, `.agents` -> `agents`. That is the whole rule, and every property this section needs falls out of it. Deterministic: the same tool on the same machine derives the same string every session, with nothing to remember and nothing to invent. Stable across model upgrades: the home does not move when the model behind it changes, which is exactly the false alarm named above. Distinct between real actors: two different tools driving one project read from two different homes, which is what the six-names-for-three-actors history actually was -- `claude-opus`, `claude-sonnet-5`, `gemini-pro` and `antigravity-gemini` collapse to `claude` and `antigravity`, and they were never four actors. Observable: the value is a directory on disk rather than a self-report, so it is the same string whether the agent is careful or careless. **A model build name is never a seat**, and `tools/validate.py` FAILs one -- a value carrying a model-family token (`opus`, `sonnet`, `gpt`, `gemini`, `deepseek`, and the rest of that closed list) names the thing that changes, not the seat that persists. This section called the stability half behavioural because nothing could tell a genuine handover from a renamed seat; deriving the value removes most of that question rather than answering it. **Degraded path**, named rather than left to improvisation: the protocol read from a bare clone with no agent home identifiable -- use the platform's own canonical name, and LOG a `DEC` saying it was self-reported rather than derived, so the one case a checker cannot distinguish is at least visible in history. **subSaipen states are outside this**: a sub writes its own slug (`saiwiki`, `saipython`), which is its identity by construction and already unique per sub. **Known limit, stated rather than papered over**: two sessions of the SAME tool on one project derive the same seat and are indistinguishable -- which is the concurrency case the Concurrency boundary below already puts outside Core's envelope, and the parallel-session damage this repository actually recorded (E-1863) was two different tools, which this does separate. Placeholders (`id`, `<name>`, `AgentID`, `unknown`) are never valid values -- `tools/validate.py` FAILs them.
- **Attempt follows claim; claim follows closed attempt (T-1148).** Claiming
  a ticket while an Attempt episode is open on it is refused: the successor
  first closes the predecessor's dangling episode (`result interrupted`,
  `stop unknown` unless the real stop reason is recorded), then claims, then
  opens its own episode. A ticket MUST NOT finish (`ticket done`) while its
  producing episode is open -- the producer closes it first, normally as
  `result candidate` / `stop completed_execution`, which is a CLAIM that its
  output is ready for admission, never the admission itself.
- **Concurrency boundary**: Core's safety model is claim-serialized tickets with ONE agent writing `.saipen/` at any instant. Two-plus agents making uncoordinated concurrent checkpoints is outside Core's envelope -- `E-###` allocation races and last-writer-wins `STATE.md` are exactly why (the validator catches that wreckage after the fact; nothing in Core prevents it up front, by design -- SPEC.md: a state protocol, not a distributed consensus algorithm). An agent that observes `STATE.md` with `agent:` other than itself and `updated:` fresher than the claim window MUST assume a live concurrent session: work only tickets it claims under this section's rules.
- **Producer parallelism (V7, T-1100)**: Isolated SubSaipen PRODUCER roles (`saitranslate`, `saiwiki`) MAY prepare READY packages in parallel -- cross-producer (`saitranslate` + `saiwiki`) concurrently, same-producer serialized by a producer-local `ProducerLock` -- WITHOUT Concurrent Mode and WITHOUT any second canonical Core writer. Core remains the sole main-tree writer; `saipen crew` stays serial (§ 1.10); producer prepare stays read-only to the main tree; integration/collect/disposition/ship stay serialized through Core's canonical `project_writer_lock`. This eliminates five defect classes: (1) **one producer corrupting another's package** -- packages are content-hash validated (never mtime) and a crash leaves only incomplete staging, never a false READY; (2) **a producer forging readiness** -- `package_identity` is deterministic over `base_source_head + base_source_tree_fingerprint + role_revision + read_set + write_set + requested_scope`, and duplicate prepares reuse the READY record (idempotent) instead of duplicating; (3) **a stale worker publishing** -- each producer namespace carries a monotonic epoch; a worker whose epoch was superseded by a takeover cannot publish READY; (4) **a producer mutating Core STATE/BOARD/LOG or shipping** -- `assert_producer_capability` / `guard_core_mutation` refuse Core STATE/BOARD/LOG mutation, integration, collect/disposition, commit/tag/push, ship with `CAPABILITY_DENIED` and zero writes; (5) **two independently-prepared packages falsely flagged whole-tree STALE when deps don't conflict** -- integration revalidates `read_set` + `write_set` against live content hashes: identical global source identity = CURRENT fast path; non-identical but content-stable = COMPATIBLE_DRIFT (usable); only a changed read or diverged write = STALE (reprepare). `A.write ∩ B.write`, `A.write ∩ B.read`, `B.write ∩ A.read` are derived explicitly and the exact conflict reason is exposed. Full decision and test matrix in `KNOWLEDGE/ADR-0001-v7-producer-parallelism.md`; implementation in `tools/saipen_engine/producer.py` (`ProducerPackage`, `classify_integration`, `derive_conflicts`, `ProducerEpoch`, `StagingGeneration`, `plan_integration`, `integrate_packages_core`), `capability.py` (`assert_producer_capability`, `guard_core_mutation`), `lock.py` (`ProducerLock`, `producer_writer_lock`); conformance in `tools/test_v7_producer_parallelism.py` (matrix A–N, 14/14). NON-GOALS preserved: no `saipen concurrent`, no distributed STATE/BOARD/LOG, no Core worktrees, no release-captain/takeover/deadlock machinery, no change to `saipen crew` semantics, no DB/daemon.
- **Whole-STATE phase claims go stale the same way, not just BOARD tickets.** A parallel instance (`TRANSLATE`'s own dedicated-agent mode, § 2.1/`phases/translate.md`) is explicitly told not to write the shared `phase:` field -- but that rule is textual, same as every other isolation rule in this document (§ 1.9's extension boundary, the subSaipen boundary `extensions/subs/PROTOCOL.md` § 1 states), so a weak or confused instance can and does violate it anyway. (`tests/scenarios/parallel-translate-isolation/` records the incident.) Finding `STATE.md` in a phase you never personally entered, under an `agent:` you don't recognize as yourself, with no matching `LOG.md` activity since -> treat it exactly like a stale `## DOING` claim above: LOG a `DEC` line naming the old agent and the observed staleness, inspect what it actually produced (`.saipen/saitranslate/kitchen/` for a real partial bundle, or the shared files themselves for direct damage) before rebuilding `STATE.md` to reflect your own real continuation -- never silently overwrite without recording the takeover, and never silently keep working under a phase you never set yourself.

### 1.5 Checkpointing & Recovery
- **Checkpointing**: MUST checkpoint after every ticket, after every phase transition, and before session termination. The phase-transition trigger is not redundant with the per-ticket one: a single ticket walks `SCOUT -> BUILD -> VERIFY -> REVIEW`, and without it a crash anywhere in that walk leaves `STATE.phase` naming a phase the work has already left, which Recovery then has to reconstruct from the LOG instead of simply reading. Three separate files cannot be written as one atomic transaction, so order stands in for atomicity: **(1)** append the `LOG.md` event, **(2)** write `BOARD.md` (temp file + rename), **(3)** write `STATE.md` (temp file + rename) *last* -- `STATE.md` is the commit pointer. That last write MUST use current `schema_version: 3`, set `last_event` to the highest `E-###` now present across sealed plus active LOG (only a fresh empty LOG omits it), and set `style_contract` to `STYLE.md`'s declared marker. A crash between any two steps always leaves `STATE.md` truthfully behind `LOG.md`/`BOARD.md`, never ahead of them, which is exactly the condition Recovery below already knows how to fix. **The LOG append itself is a UTF-8 write.** Windows PowerShell 5.1's `Add-Content`/`Set-Content` -- even with `-Encoding utf8` -- re-encodes non-ASCII through the console codepage and writes invalid UTF-8, corrupting the file the validator then fails (`KNOWLEDGE/traps.md` names the trap). Append with the editor tools, or in one of these byte-safe forms: PowerShell `[System.IO.File]::AppendAllText('.saipen/LOG.md', $line + [Environment]::NewLine, (New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding($false)))`; bash `printf '%s\n' 'LINE' >> .saipen/LOG.md`; Python `open('.saipen/LOG.md', 'a', encoding='utf-8').write(line + '\n')`. Writing `STATE.md` first (or in any other order) can leave it claiming something `BOARD.md` doesn't yet reflect -- a crash Recovery would not reliably catch.
- **Every one of the three writes is confirmed by reading it back, not just `STATE.md`.** § 1.4 already requires re-reading `BOARD.md` after writing a *claim*; the same duty covers writing *content* -- a new ticket, a status move, a `## DONE` line. After step (2), re-read `BOARD.md` and confirm the tickets you meant to write are actually on it; after step (1), confirm the LOG line is the last line of the file. A real incident, in the session that shipped this rule: an agent wrote fourteen tickets to `BOARD.md` through a pattern substitution whose anchor did not match the file, then printed its own hardcoded success message and moved on. Eleven tickets of work were done with the board silently unchanged -- every edit landed, only the bookkeeping vanished, and nothing noticed for an hour because the *validator* stayed green: an empty `## TODO` is perfectly conformant. This is the general shape and it is worth naming: **a write you did not read back is a claim, and a success message your own tool printed is not evidence.** The three-file order (§ 1.5) protects against a crash between writes; it does nothing about a write that quietly did not happen.
- **Confirm the checkpoint you just wrote, don't assume it landed well-formed.** After step (3), re-read `STATE.md` and check it still carries **every field § 1.2's required set names** -- go read that list there, do not work from a copy held anywhere else, including a copy in your own context from earlier in the session. This section deliberately does not restate the fields -- its own copy once omitted `transition_from`, so an agent self-checking against it confirmed states the validator then failed. Where a validator is reachable, `tools/validate.py` IS this check and running it is both cheaper and more trustworthy than reading nine field names off a page. This is the same re-read-after-write discipline § 1.4 already mandates for a claim ("after writing a claim, re-read `BOARD.md` and confirm your claim actually survived"); the failure mode is identical, and until v7.84.0 only one of the two had the guard. A checkpoint written without `next_action` fails TEST-001 outright -- the one guarantee this protocol exists to make (`tests/scenarios/checkpoint-self-confirmation/`). Missing or empty required field -> fix it before doing anything else; a checkpoint that cannot be resumed from is not a checkpoint. Where a validator is reachable, running it is the cheapest way to do this check and SHOULD be preferred over eyeballing.
- **Dirty tree on continuation**: uncommitted project changes at cold-start are a NORMAL protocol state, never an anomaly to warn the user about -- work commits at `SHIP`, not per checkpoint, so an in-flight ticket's edits are uncommitted by design. Before touching anything, the agent MUST attribute them: cross-reference `git status`/`git diff --stat` against the open `DOING` ticket's scope, the `LOG.md` tail, and `kitchen/`. No git available (`mode: no-publish`, or no repo at all) -- there is no "uncommitted" concept to diff against, but the same attribution question still matters (did *I* leave this mid-edit, or did someone else touch it between sessions): fall back to filesystem modification timestamps on the changed-looking files themselves, cross-referenced against `STATE.md`'s own `updated:` and the open `DOING` ticket's scope, the same mtime heuristic Recovery below already uses for the no-git case. Attributable to the in-flight ticket -> that IS its work so far: continue or adopt it (§ 1.4) and let the changes fold into its eventual commit, no ceremony. Not attributable to any ticket (the user's own edits, another tool's output) -> the agent MUST NOT commit, revert, stash, or clean it: reverting is user-data deletion (§ 1.1), committing stamps authorship over changes nobody reviewed. Only when unattributed changes sit in the very files the next action must touch does the agent stop and ask -- that is § 1.1's existing destructive-op confirmation gate, not a new `WAIT:` category.
- **Recovery**: **Under `mode: read-only`, Recovery MUST NOT write anything** -- rebuilding `STATE.md` is a file write, and that mode is set precisely because writes are unavailable, so the whole procedure below is unexecutable there. Report the stale or corrupt state in chat, name the exact repair that would be required (which field, rebuilt to what value, from which evidence), and stop. Do not perform a partial repair, and do not report a rebuild that did not happen. This is the same resolution § 1.3 gives for `mode:` itself and § 1.10 gives for `saipen stop`'s digest: the honest branch, not a silent skip. With writes available: if `STATE.md` is stale but `LOG.md` or `BOARD.md` is newer, `git status` is ground truth where git is available. Without git (`mode: no-publish`, § 1.3, or no repo at all) -- fall back to filesystem modification timestamps on `LOG.md`/`BOARD.md`/`STATE.md` themselves: whichever of the three was written most recently, cross-referenced against the Checkpointing write order above (LOG, then BOARD, then STATE last), tells you how far a crash got without needing git at all. One § 1.4 caveat on that heuristic: a standalone `claim_time` refresh legitimately touches `BOARD.md` with no `STATE.md` write after it, mimicking a crash between checkpoint steps 2 and 3 -- before rebuilding anything, check whether `BOARD.md`'s difference from what `STATE.md`/`LOG.md` imply is claim fields only; if so, that is a live claim being kept fresh, not a crash. **Recovery MUST be idempotent -- running it twice changes nothing the first run did not already do.** `continue -> crash -> continue -> crash -> continue` is the expected life of an autonomous run, so every step here has to be safe to repeat: rebuilding an already-rebuilt `STATE.md` yields the same file, re-adopting an already-adopted ticket is a no-op rather than a second claim, and the recovery copy below is written under a timestamped name precisely so a second pass adds a file instead of overwriting the evidence the first pass saved. Recovery that is only safe the first time is worse than none, because the crash it exists to survive is exactly what invokes it repeatedly. Where a step cannot be made naturally repeatable, it MUST be made *detectably* repeatable -- check whether the work is already done before doing it, the same shape as the sealing idempotency check in § 1.2.
Before overwriting a corrupt or stale `STATE.md`, copy it to `.saipen/recovery/<timestamp>-STATE.md` -- Recovery reconstructs from evidence, it does not destroy evidence. Rebuild `STATE.md` based on latest `LOG.md` entry and open DOING ticket. **Neither input is guaranteed present, and the checkpoint order is why**: LOG is written first, so a crash between steps 1 and 2 leaves a LOG line about a ticket `BOARD.md` still shows in `## TODO`. An absent `## DOING` therefore does not mean nothing was in flight -- scan the LOG tail for the most recent `[T-###]` with no later `DONE`/ship line closing it, and resume that, re-claiming it properly (§ 1.4). **A live `## DOING` ticket outranks the LOG tail, whatever the tail says.** Check `BOARD.md` first: any ticket in `## DOING` is the thing that was in flight, and Recovery rebuilds around it (adopting it per § 1.4 if unclaimed) -- the LOG-tail heuristics here exist only to answer "what was in flight" when `## DOING` cannot. Ordering matters because the two disagree in the ordinary case, not a rare one: the checkpoint writes LOG first, so any session-level event after a ticket's last line makes the tail look ticket-less while the board still holds the real answer. And when `## DOING` is empty *and* the latest LOG entry is session-level rather than ticket-level (`T-none`: a `SHIP`, `CLEAN`, `MARKHUNT`, `VALIDATE`, or a bare `DEC`), there is no ticket to infer and none should be invented: rebuild to `phase: DONE` with `task: none` and let `phases/done.md`'s own priority logic pick the next real work from `BOARD.md`, which is the same place a clean session would have landed anyway. If the recovered `STATE.md` shows (or `LOG.md` shows a `DEC` pivot line for) `execution_intent: goal`, `goal_waves`/`goal_tickets` MUST be rebuilt too -- count wave/ticket-completion events since the NEWEST goal marker, never assume `0`. Two lines are markers, not one: the `DEC` pivot line for `saipen goal <text>`, and the `DEC: goal reauthorized` line § 2.4 requires from a re-authorizing `cc`. Counting from the pivot alone rebuilds every bump a later re-authorization already cancelled, which hands the run back a tripped valve the human cleared. Only increments are completion events -- a marker's own `N->0` records the reset, it is not a wave or a ticket. The pivot line itself may have been sealed away (§ 1.2) if enough LOG activity happened since -- the active `LOG.md` alone is not guaranteed to still contain it, so Recovery MUST walk back into `.saipen/logs/LOG-<NNN>.md` segments (in `NNN` order) when the pivot line or the required count isn't found in the active file alone; this is exactly the "parent chain or an audit actually walks back into them" case § 1.2 already carves out as the reason sealed segments exist at all. Losing the count on recovery is exactly the failure § 2.4's safety valve exists to prevent.

Any Recovery rebuild MUST also migrate the Core state to `schema_version: 3`, set `last_event` to the highest real `E-###` found across sealed plus active LOG, omitting it only when that complete sequence is empty, and set `style_contract` from `STYLE.md`. The value comes from evidence, never from `updated:` or BOARD; rebuilding the same evidence therefore produces the same marker and remains idempotent.

### 1.6 Core State Machine & Ticket DAG
`INIT → PLAN → SCOUT → BUILD → VERIFY → REVIEW → SHIP → DONE | BLOCKED`

`SHIP` has one narrow backward edge: a fixable preflight failure found before
commit/tag/push returns the current ticket to `BUILD`, then repeats
`VERIFY -> REVIEW -> SHIP`. It is not a retry path for a failed publish and
never permits editing after a successful push (`phases/ship.md`).

- **Ticket DAG**: Tickets MUST define dependencies using `needs: [T-XXX]`. **Pick Rule**: a `## TODO` ticket is *workable* when all its `needs:` are marked `DONE`, it is not under another agent's active claim (§ 1.4), and it carries no `blocker:` field. The last condition is defense in depth against malformed input: full validation already rejects `## TODO` plus `blocker:`, but router and claim MUST still refuse it, and an explicit priority override MUST NOT override authorization. **Agents MUST take the topmost workable ticket, reading `## TODO` from the top.** Eligibility alone left the actual choice open, which is a hole in a section named Determinism Invariants: two conformant agents handed the same board could pick different tickets and both be right. Board order is priority -- § 2.4's Entry already relies on exactly that when it inserts a new objective's tickets at the top -- so the first workable line wins, and re-prioritising means moving lines, not arguing about them.
- **VERIFY**: MUST be executed. Failure retries within VERIFY itself, not a phase transition -- `phases/verify.md`'s own cap (3 dead hypotheses or 2 failed fix cycles) moves the ticket to `## BLOCKED` on `BOARD.md` and the agent picks up other workable tickets instead. Success transitions to `REVIEW`.
- **MANUAL-VERIFY**: If `mode: manual-verify`, `VERIFY` MUST block and await human confirmation. Agent MUST NOT auto-transition to `REVIEW`.
- **DONE**: A ticket MUST NOT be marked `DONE` without a successful `VERIFY` (or human `MANUAL-VERIFY`).

**Work vs Attempt (T-1148).** The ticket is the durable Work; an Attempt is
one bounded execution episode of one agent working it. An Attempt may fail,
be interrupted, yield, or be superseded WITHOUT renaming, failing or
destroying the Work -- the successor re-claims the same ticket and continues.
The chain a completion claim must walk is always
`claim -> evidence -> verdict -> transition`: a producer's `candidate`
episode, its `RUN:` lines and its own assertions are CLAIMS; only
verification evidence recorded after the claim (`phases/verify.md`'s
boundary + PASS/MANUAL-VERIFY grammar, the same one `ticket done` already
requires) is EVIDENCE, and the independent gates (VERIFY -> REVIEW -> SHIP)
are the VERDICT that may authorize the TRANSITION to `## DONE`. A producer
MUST NOT close its own Work, and `tools/validate.py` FAILs a DONE ticket
whose latest producing episode closed `candidate` with no VERIFY boundary
after that close -- retroactive self-admission is fabrication, not
completion.

**Phase enum**: `INIT`, `PLAN`, `SCOUT`, `BUILD`, `VERIFY`, `REVIEW`, `SHIP`, `DONE`, `VALIDATE`, `HUNT`, `MARKHUNT`, `ADD`, `CLEAN`, `TRANSLATE`, `PREPARE`, `BLOCKED`. These sixteen are deliberately factored, not accidentally many: the 2-tier loader (`KNOWLEDGE/decisions.md`, v5.0.0) loads only the active phase's own `phases/*.md`, so the *count* is nearly free — merging them into fewer fat docs raises per-call tokens, it doesn't lower them. Recurring "collapse the phases into 4/5/8" proposals have been rejected with full reasons; read `KNOWLEDGE/decisions.md` before re-proposing one.

**Transition table** -- the single canonical transition source. This table is authoritative for every phase transition; no phase document may override, add, or remove an exit listed here. Phase documents may explain and constrain their phase's behaviour but MUST match the exits in this matrix. A phase document whose stated transitions contradict this table is in violation, and `tools/validate.py` FAILs the mismatch. **Every `BLOCKED` in this table means `STATE.phase: BLOCKED`** (`phases/blocked.md`) -- the DFA this table describes has no other kind of state. `## BLOCKED` on `BOARD.md` (§ 1.2) is a completely different, ticket-level concept that shares the name but never appears as a `phase:` value anywhere; do not conflate the two.

```text
INIT      -> PLAN | BLOCKED
PLAN      -> SCOUT | BUILD | DONE | BLOCKED
SCOUT     -> BUILD | BLOCKED
BUILD     -> VERIFY | BLOCKED
VERIFY    -> REVIEW | SCOUT | BUILD | BLOCKED
REVIEW    -> SHIP | BUILD | SCOUT | BLOCKED
SHIP      -> DONE | BUILD | BLOCKED
DONE      -> SCOUT | PLAN | HUNT | BLOCKED
VALIDATE  -> SCOUT | PLAN | DONE | BLOCKED
HUNT      -> ADD | PLAN | SCOUT | BLOCKED
MARKHUNT  -> DONE | BLOCKED
ADD       -> BUILD | PLAN | SCOUT | DONE | BLOCKED
CLEAN     -> DONE | BLOCKED
TRANSLATE -> DONE | BLOCKED
PREPARE   -> DONE | BLOCKED
BLOCKED   -> PLAN | SCOUT | DONE
```

- **From-any-phase set**: `CLEAN`, `TRANSLATE`, `PREPARE`, `VALIDATE`, `MARKHUNT`, `PLAN`, `HUNT`.
  These seven are entered by explicit user command (§ 1.10) from ANY phase, so the transition table's FROM row does not restrict them -- the same way `saipen stop` isn't chain-specific. Once entered, their own row above governs what they can transition to next. `PLAN` is `saipen plan`, and also the `PLAN` that § 2.4's goal-mode Entry mandates on every pivot.

  **A command recognized from any phase is not the same thing as a phase reachable from any phase, and `SHIP` is where the two must not be confused.** `saipen ship` is recognized from anywhere (§ 1.10), but `phase: SHIP` is enterable **only from `REVIEW`** -- this table's single route into it, which § 1.10 states in the same words. `SHIP` was listed here as an any-phase transition from v7.83.0 (validator) and v7.92.0 (this text) until v7.94.0, so the two halves of the protocol disagreed about whether an agent could jump straight to publishing: one said run the gates, the other said the jump was a legal transition. Invoking the command from outside `REVIEW` means "take this work through the missing gates, then ship", and the `next_action` it writes is the next gate on that ticket's Core chain -- never `PHASE SHIP` until `REVIEW` has actually passed.
  This list said five until v7.92.0 while § 1.10 had listed seven the whole time, and the gap was not cosmetic: `saipen goal` MUST run `PLAN` for the new objective from wherever the pivot happens, so a pivot out of `REVIEW` -- a phase whose row allows only `SHIP`/`BUILD`/`SCOUT`/`BLOCKED` -- produced a `STATE.md` that `tools/validate.py` correctly rejected while the agent was doing exactly what § 2.4 ordered. Two rules, both followed, one invalid state. Found by the validator on a live pivot, which is the check working, not failing.
- `HUNT -> PLAN | SCOUT` (the findings case, distinct from the clean-board case which always goes to `ADD`) is stated explicitly in `phases/hunt.md` itself: findings that get ticketed go to `PLAN`, or straight to `SCOUT` for a small/obvious one.
- `DONE` never targets `ADD` directly -- `phases/done.md` itself says plainly "`ADD` itself is only ever reached autonomously via a clean `HUNT`" (§ 2.1). The only door into `ADD` is `HUNT`'s clean-sweep result; an earlier version of this table listed `ADD` as a direct `DONE` target, which no phase doc ever actually implemented.
- `ADD` never targets `HUNT` either -- the direction is `HUNT` -> `ADD` (a clean sweep opens `ADD`), never the reverse. `phases/add.md` states outright that bouncing `ADD` back to `HUNT` is illegal (it would just re-derive HUNT's "no new signal" result and loop): a bug `ADD` spots goes `RETURN SCOUT`, not `RETURN HUNT`. `ADD` begins again only after the *next* clean `HUNT` (§ 2.2), which is `HUNT` reaching `ADD`, not `ADD` reaching `HUNT`. An earlier version of this table listed `HUNT` as a direct `ADD` target -- the same phantom as the `DONE -> ADD` one above, no phase doc ever implemented it, removed for the same reason.
- **A repeated attempt MUST be able to name what changed.** Before trying the same thing a second time -- a failed build, a failed `verify:`, a push that did not land, a hypothesis already rejected -- the agent MUST be able to state, in the LOG line that records the attempt, what is different from last time: new evidence, a changed input, a different environment, a narrowed hypothesis. **If the honest answer is "nothing", the retry is forbidden** -- move the ticket to `## BLOCKED` with `| blocker:` naming the dead end, or pick different work. Counters alone do not carry this: `phases/verify.md`'s 3-hypothesis/2-fix-cycle cap and `phases/ship.md`'s retry-once both bound how MANY times an attempt repeats, and neither asks whether the second attempt could possibly go differently. `phases/verify.md` has said "never re-test without new evidence" of debugging hypotheses since long before this clause; it was true of every repeated attempt and stated for one of them, which is the same fixed-where-noticed shape this protocol keeps finding in itself. Identical retries are the cheapest way for an autonomous run to burn its whole budget while producing nothing, and a goal run makes them free to start and invisible to the human until the valve trips.
  - This is behavioural: no artifact in `.saipen/` witnesses whether the agent actually had new information, so `tools/validate.py` cannot enforce it, and CONFORMANCE records it as a stated limit rather than an implied guarantee. What it can do is refuse the excuse -- the LOG line for a repeated attempt names the delta, so a human or a later agent reading the graph can see whether one existed.
- `VERIFY -> SCOUT | BUILD` and `REVIEW -> SCOUT | BUILD` do NOT mean the ticket that just failed VERIFY or REVIEW bounces back for a retry -- both phase docs send that specific ticket to `BLOCKED` when their own cap trips (`phases/verify.md`'s 3-hypothesis/2-fix-cycle cap, `phases/review.md`'s 2-pass cap), then pick up a *different*, unblocked `TODO` ticket, landing in `SCOUT` or `BUILD` for that one. `v7.18.0` removed a real same-ticket bounce-back bug from this exact row; these targets are the later, distinct "switch to other work" addition, not a regression of that fix.
- **`-> BLOCKED` is universal, not per-phase vocabulary.** Every row above ends in `| BLOCKED` because *any* phase can turn out to be genuinely stuck, not because each phase doc separately invents its own trigger for it. Several docs do name a concrete one (`phases/clean.md`'s unsafe deletion candidate, `phases/prepare.md`'s unrepairable staleness, `phases/done.md`'s all-tickets-unworkable, `phases/verify.md`/`review.md`'s caps); the ones that don't (`add.md`, `hunt.md`, `markhunt.md`, `translate.md`) are not thereby forbidden from reaching it -- a phase that cannot proceed and has no other workable ticket goes `BLOCKED` with the facts, which is `phases/blocked.md`'s own entry condition and needs no per-doc restatement. Do not read a phase doc's silence here as "this phase must push through no matter what."
- `saipen status` MUST NOT change `phase` -- it is read-only (§ 1.10).
- `saipen stop` checkpoints and halts; it is not itself a phase transition (§ 1.10).
- Maintenance phases (`HUNT`/`ADD`/`CLEAN`/`TRANSLATE`) MUST NOT be required for Core continuation -- the Core chain (`INIT` through `DONE`/`BLOCKED`) is fully self-contained; a cold agent running Core alone never needs to reach them.

### 1.7 Workspace Hygiene
The protocol lives in the SAIPEN home; the project holds work, not protocol copies.
- `saipen set` in project root MUST NOT copy phases or scripts into the project. It MUST write a bootloader to `.saipen/STATE.md` pointing to the canonical `saipen/` home path -- concretely, the `saipen_home` frontmatter field (§ 1.2): the absolute path this bootstrap read the protocol from. A successor agent on the same machine finds the protocol through it; a successor on a different machine that finds the path dead installs/clones SAIPEN at an explicit candidate path and runs the ONE mechanical rebind operation `saipen rebind-home <candidate>` — it proves the candidate (readable `VERSION`, compatible major, `BOOT` layout, required protocol files), journals one narrowly-owned `STATE.saipen_home` pointer update with truthful LOG evidence, and preserves phase/task/board. The engine-free fallback (clone + hand-edit the field) is for hosts without Python/SAIOPS only. (This field existed only as an unimplemented sentence in this section until v7.25.0 -- states written before then legally lack it.)
- Phase transitions MUST load the authoritative Markdown files directly from the global `saipen/phases/` directory via absolute path.

### 1.8 Batch Input Parsing (The "No Rush" Rule)
If the user provides a raw list or backlog of multiple features, tasks, or bug reports, the agent MUST NOT attempt to implement them all in a single pass. The agent MUST parse the list into individual `TODO` tickets on `BOARD.md` and execute them surgically, strictly one by one. "One by one" governs BUILD scope -- one ticket's changes per edit pass, never several tickets' code mixed into one -- not cadence. It does NOT mean pausing between tickets: under `execution_intent: goal` (§ 2.4) tickets still flow without stopping; outside it, the normal per-ticket checkpoint (§ 1.5) still applies exactly as always.

- **Trailing Extrapolation ('etc.')**: If a user prompt, backlog list, or feature request contains "etc.", "etcetera", "and so on", "и т.д.", or similar phrasing, the agent MUST interpret it as an explicit instruction for "similar additions" — inferring and creating tickets for obvious missing workflow steps, complementary features, or logical extensions required to complete the pattern (aligning with § 2.3).

### 1.9 Extension Discovery
- `.saipen/extensions/<name>/` **inside the consuming project's own `.saipen/`** is an optional, project-attached phase hook (e.g. `security`, `performance`) that the project's own author creates and maintains -- `saipen set` / `init.md` and the injector do NOT auto-populate it, the same way they never auto-populate a project's `tests/`. Each extension's own `README.md` states which phase it attaches to and what it requires there. Everything protocol-shaped a project carries lives under one `.saipen/` roof -- not scattered as sibling dot-and-plain folders across the project root (v7.35.0; before then the attachment point was a root-level `extensions/`). **Legacy**: a project bootstrapped earlier MAY still carry root-level `extensions/<name>/` -- agents MUST recognize it as equivalent, and MAY migrate it (`git mv extensions .saipen/extensions`, one LOG line) at a convenient checkpoint; never maintain both locations at once. **Both exist at once** (a partial/failed migration, or two agents disagreeing) is a conflict, not a merge job: treat `.saipen/extensions/<name>/` as authoritative, the root-level copy as stale, ticket its removal, and do not silently combine or guess which copy is newer.
- On entering a phase, the agent MUST check whether the *project's* `.saipen/extensions/<name>/` (or legacy root `extensions/<name>/`) exists for that phase before proceeding. If it exists, its README's instructions apply for that phase. If it is absent, the phase proceeds exactly as its `phases/*.md` doc describes, with zero extension overhead. Absence MUST NOT block a phase transition -- extensions layer on top of Core, they never gate it.
- If an extension IS present but its own stated requirements aren't met on this host (a scanner binary missing, an API key unset) -- and Core's own safety/correctness isn't at stake -- the agent MUST degrade: skip what that requirement blocks, note it plainly in the phase's findings, and continue. If proceeding without it would be genuinely unsafe (the extension exists specifically because this project needs that check, and it silently can't run), the agent MUST go `BLOCKED` instead of pretending the check happened. Either way, an extension MUST NOT overwrite or weaken Core normative behavior -- it can only add checks on top of Core, never relax what Core already requires.
- The `extensions/security/` and `extensions/performance/` folders inside the SAIPEN home itself are reference *examples* for a project author to copy and adapt -- the same role `extensions/templates/` plays for `.saipen/` boilerplate. The home's top-level `extensions/` is the shipped LIBRARY (what the injector distributes and `saipen sub spawn` bootstraps from) and keeps that location; only the *attachment point inside a consuming project* is `.saipen/extensions/`. They are not live hooks the SAIPEN home reads on its own behalf; nothing discovers them unless a project author has replicated the pattern into their own project.
- `extensions/schemas/` is a distinct, non-behavioral case: descriptive JSON Schemas held for a future external validator (see `extensions/schemas/README.md`). It is not a phase hook and this section does not apply to it. **Exception**: `state.schema.json` is live, not reference -- `tools/validate.py` reads it directly to check `STATE.md`'s shape (CONFORMANCE.md § 1). `board.schema.json`/`log.schema.json` remain descriptive-only, unread by any agent today; don't assume the same of `state.schema.json` just because they share a directory.
- An extension MAY define its own bare-command vocabulary (e.g. `saipen sub spawn <name>`) in its own README/PROTOCOL doc -- recognized ONLY while that extension's folder exists in the project, and MUST NOT collide with a § 1.10 name. These commands are never registered in § 1.10's closed list; § 1.10 alone still governs what's recognized with no extension present. This is the general mechanism -- no extension gets bespoke Core wording for its own commands. **Two active extensions defining the same word** (both attached to this project, e.g. two independently-authored phase hooks that happened to pick an identical bare-command) is never silently resolved by picking whichever loads first -- decline, name both extensions and the colliding word, and ask the user which one they meant. This is the same "never guess" floor § 1.9's own degradation rule already applies elsewhere, just for a collision between two extensions instead of one extension against Core.

### 1.10 Command Surface
The complete set of recognized user-facing commands. Phase-affecting ones are defined in full where cited; `status` and `stop` are defined here because they are meta/control operations, not phase transitions -- they can be invoked from any phase and MUST NOT be treated as work themselves.
- `saipen set` / `saipen init` -- bootstrap `.saipen/` (§ 1.7, `phases/init.md`).
- `saipen continue` / bare `saipen` / `cc` -- read `STATE.md`/`BOARD.md`/tail of `LOG.md`, execute `next_action` immediately, no rebriefing. **The persisted `execution_intent` decides what a resume means** (MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4, the convergence contract). With `execution_intent: goal`, continue resumes the existing goal exactly as MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4 Entry's bare form describes -- the objective is preserved, never replaced or re-planned merely because this key was typed, and a tripped safety valve is re-authorized (both counters reset to `0`) exactly as MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4 Entry conditions the reset on the valve having tripped. With `execution_intent: converge`, continue resumes convergence. With `execution_intent: normal`, bare `cc` ENTERS converge: it exhausts existing real work and maintenance to the closure contract -- never ADD, never invented features -- and it NEVER asks for an objective. `cc <arguments>` is not a goal and is never guessed into one: it replies exactly `Use: gg <objective>` and stops (§ 1.1, MAINTENANCE.md § 2.1 DEFAULT BEHAVIOR, `CONFORMANCE.md` TEST-001). **A resume MUST also name what is stuck**, in its reply, in one line: scan `BOARD.md`'s `## BLOCKED` section for every blocked ticket, any untriaged `[MARKHUNT]` finding, and a live `WAIT:` if one is being carried. Report only -- no new field, no state change, nothing queued. `.saipen/kitchen/digest.md` carries this at `saipen stop` and `SHIP` and nowhere else, so a blocked ticket could sit unmentioned across an unbounded number of sessions while every one of them truthfully reported the work it did do. The human's whole view of what is parked came from a file written only when the run ended.
- `saipen goal <text>` / `gg <objective>` -- pivot to a NEW objective, run to completion (MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4). Bare `saipen goal` / `gg` with no text is a usage line, never a continuation alias, never a resume: it replies exactly `Use: gg <objective text>` and stops -- it MUST NOT set the goal intent, MUST NOT write or reset either counter, MUST NOT plan, MUST NOT resume, and MUST NOT guess an objective from `BOARD.md`. One line, no state change, which also satisfies § 1.11's "a session that did nothing MUST say exactly that." The failure mode worth naming is the tempting one: an agent that treats bare `gg` as a generic "start goal mode" flips the intent and initializes `goal_waves`/`goal_tickets` at `0`, handing a full safety-valve budget to a run nobody authorized -- the same accounting loss the conditional reset in MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4 Entry exists to prevent. **One carve-out, and it is a pair rather than a loosening of the bare form.** A bare goal key standing in the SAME message immediately after a plan command (`dd gg`, or `saipen plan` then `saipen goal`) takes the plan that command just wrote as its objective: `execution_intent: goal`, counters from `0`, straight into `SCOUT` for the topmost workable ticket, exactly as MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4 Entry's with-text form. The objective IS stated -- it is the plan now sitting on `BOARD.md`, which the user asked for in the same breath -- so the thing this ban protects is present rather than missing. What the ban actually guards against is E-1468: a lone bare key typed out of habit mid-run, handing itself a fresh 3-wave/20-ticket budget for an objective nobody stated. That is not a key pressed immediately after asking for a plan, and a bare goal command on its own is unchanged in every respect. Without this the shortcut table invites a combination that can never complete -- `dd` ends Proposal Mode at `execution_intent: normal`, where the next key is not a command at all -- which is CONFORMANCE 207's defect at the level of a PAIR instead of a row.
- `saipen plan [text]` -- explicit trigger for PLAN phase (`phases/plan.md`).
- `saipen clean` -- explicit CLEAN trigger (`phases/clean.md`).
- `saipen translate` -- isolated translation build (`phases/translate.md`).
- `saipen markhunt` -- dry exhaustive audit, records findings to BOARD.md, never fixes anything (`phases/markhunt.md`).
- `saipen prepare [producer]` -- force-fresh regenerate and verify the named producer's package for handoff (`phases/prepare.md`); an old ready package is not reused without the producer's deterministic byte/content-equivalent cache proof.
- `saipen collect <producer>` -- consume exactly one named producer's already-prepared handoff. `saitranslate` reads `.saipen/saitranslate/kitchen/OUTBOX.md`; a subSaipen such as `saiwiki` reads `.saipen/extensions/subs/<producer>/kitchen/OUTBOX.md`. Before any main-project write, run `tools/validate.py --gate collect:<producer>` -- the producer-hard gate, and the only place a package's readiness is a hard FAIL outside CONVERGE.md stage M. It requires `status: ready` and exact matches to the current `source_head + source_tree_fingerprint + role_revision`, plus complete `coverage`, an explicit `payload`, passing producer-side `verified` evidence, and executable `instructions`; then apply only that payload, create/claim a normal Core ticket, and enter its ordinary `VERIFY -> REVIEW -> SHIP` chain. Core is a consumer: it MUST NOT edit, revalidate, refresh, or otherwise bless freshness evidence on an old package. ANY mismatch means REFUSE, mark the package stale, and run producer preparation again; only that producer may regenerate/reverify and write a fresh package. A missing, draft, blocked, stale, already-reviewed, incomplete, or unverifiable handoff is a hard no-op: for `saitranslate` reply exactly `Not ready: run ee first.`; for `saiwiki` reply exactly `Not ready: run qq first.`; for `saitest` reply exactly `Not ready: run saitest first.`; for another producer name it in the same form. **`saitest` is invoked by its bare sub name rather than a repeated-letter key** -- `tt` belongs to `saipen test`, which runs a project's declared suite and is a different command -- and the bare-subname route is the one `extensions/subs/README.md` already defines for every `sai*` sub: one word adopts the role, spawning it first if needed. Naming the refusal after the thing that clears it is the whole point of these strings, so this one names the sub, not a key that would send the reader to the wrong command. No main-project file, checkpoint, Git ref, or remote may change on that refusal. **An agent MUST NOT edit OUTBOX.md to change `status` from `draft`/`blocked` to `ready` unless all coverage gaps, missing locales, or stale sources named in the draft are actually resolved.** Forcing `status: ready` on incomplete coverage is a protocol violation — the collect gate reads the OUTBOX as evidence, and fabricating that evidence is indistinguishable from the producer having done the work. A user repeating the collect command is not authorization to forge the handoff; reply with the same refusal and state the exact gap that must close. Collection is targeted, never shorthand for the extension-wide `saipen sub collect`, and it MUST run the same boundary, freshness, and crash-safe write-order checks as `extensions/subs/PROTOCOL.md` § 4. `collect` integrates; it does not itself waive or perform SHIP unless a shortcut below explicitly chains SHIP after it.
- `saipen ship` -- explicit SHIP trigger (`phases/ship.md`); SHIP also fires implicitly per that phase's other stated conditions.
- `saipen hunt` -- explicit `HUNT` trigger (`phases/hunt.md`).
- `saipen validate` -- explicit VALIDATE trigger (`phases/validate.md`).
- `saipen crew` -- walk the whole circuit in a fixed order rather than driving each factory by hand: sense (`hunt`), reproduce (saitest), intake (`collect saitest`), build (the ordinary `SCOUT -> BUILD -> VERIFY -> REVIEW` chain per ticket), translate (`prepare` then `collect saitranslate`), document (`prepare` then `collect saiwiki`), publish (`ship`). It adds exactly one mechanism -- the durable orchestration target (`execution_intent: converge` with `converge_target: crew`) that makes the circuit resumable and crash-derivable from evidence rather than a dumb stage counter -- while every stage reuses commands this section already defines and the ledger is `BOARD.md`/`LOG.md`/`OUTBOX.md`. **The contract it exists for: a stage hands the next stage a reproduction or a verdict, never a claim** -- an OUTBOX entry, a ticket with its `verify:`, a LOG line carrying the command and its output; chat is not a hand-off surface. An empty stage is a result and is recorded as one; a stage that cannot finish stops the circuit with `BLOCKED` and the missing fact named, rather than passing a partial result forward with a note to be careful. It waives no gate and runs nothing in parallel -- `extensions/subs/crew.md` holds the full circuit, its hand-off rules and the incident that produced them. **`saipen crew` carries exactly one execution meaning: this strictly sequential circuit. It is never the concurrent multi-agent design** -- the future concurrent execution design is a separate concept with its own distinct command name (the v8 Concurrent Mode design in `KNOWLEDGE/crew-v8-backlog.md`), and one command may not carry both meanings (T-571).
- `saipen test` -- run the declared test suite and report PASS/FAIL per suite; MUST NOT write to any file, change phase, or fix a failing test. A failing test is a finding -- record it through the normal ticket chain, never patch it silently. Where a project names no suite, the report says so plainly. The suite is the same one the project's gate runs (`tools/run_scenarios.py`, `tools/audit_checks.py`, the portable floor, and any consumer-side `tests/`); `test` executes it and reports, `validate` repairs structural breaks -- two different jobs, and `test` does not quietly become `validate`.
- `saipen status` -- MUST read `BOARD.md` and `STATE.md` and report current phase, the in-flight ticket, and what's queued next. MUST NOT write to any file or perform any work -- read-only, no exceptions, regardless of the execution intent.
  **It MUST also answer the question the human is actually asking**, which observation says is rarely "what phase am I in" and almost always "is this in good shape, and does anything need *me*". Those two need different facts, and the second set is the one an agent otherwise reconstructs from memory -- which a cold agent cannot do at all, making it exactly the kind of thing that belongs in state rather than in a chat scrollback. So the report MUST additionally carry, each in one line, and each omitted entirely when it is empty rather than padded with "none":
  - **Waiting on you.** Every open `WAIT:` and every `## BLOCKED` ticket whose blocker names a human decision, quoted as the concrete question. This is the human's to-do list and it is the single most useful line in the report.
  - **Claimed but unproven.** Work that is done but whose `| verify:` never ran, or ran only as `conf: low`/`med` (`phases/verify.md`). "Finished" and "verified" are different states and conflating them is how a project feels healthier than it is.
  - **Conformance.** The result of the last `tools/validate.py` run if one is recorded in `LOG.md`, with its timestamp -- or plainly that none is, which is itself the answer. The record has a fixed shape (§ 1.2's `RUN: validate.py -> PASS`/`FAIL` line), so this is a lookup rather than a reading of prose; that shape exists because this line ordered the report before anything guaranteed the record. Never re-run it: `status` is read-only, and a validator run is work.
  - **Staleness.** How old `STATE.updated` is, when that gap is large enough to matter. A tidy-looking board that nobody has touched in three weeks is not the same as a tidy board from an hour ago, and the difference does not otherwise show.
  This is a *report*, never a verdict: state what is and is not established, let the human conclude. It MUST NOT pronounce the project "healthy", "ready", or "good to ship" -- an agent grading its own work is the least valuable opinion in the room. Where `.saipen/kitchen/digest.md` is a snapshot *left behind* at `ship`/`stop`, this is the same picture *asked for* live; if the digest exists and disagrees with what `status` reads off `BOARD`/`STATE` now, say so and trust the live files -- the digest is by definition older.
- `saipen stop` -- MUST checkpoint immediately per § 1.5 (flush `LOG.md`, update `BOARD.md`, set an explicit resumable `next_action`), then (over)write the 3-line human digest `.saipen/kitchen/digest.md` (`done:`/`remaining:`/`awaiting:` — same snapshot `phases/ship.md` writes, so the human reads one small file instead of the LOG), and halt and return control to the user. **Under `mode: read-only` (§ 1.3) the digest write is not merely skipped, it is impossible** -- that mode exists precisely because filesystem writes are unavailable, so this line ordered something unsatisfiable by construction until v7.92.0, and an agent that took "MUST write" literally would either fail the stop or fabricate a success. Resolve it exactly as § 1.3 resolves the identical problem for `mode:` itself: emit the same three lines (`done:`/`remaining:`/`awaiting:`) into chat instead, leave whatever `digest.md` already exists untouched, and say plainly that it was not updated. The checkpoint clause above is under the same constraint and gets the same treatment -- a read-only session cannot checkpoint at all, so it reports the resumable `next_action` it *would* have written rather than pretending one landed on disk. MUST NOT leave a ticket mid-edit. **Overrides autonomous continuation** -- it is the user's manual brake and always wins. It does NOT change the execution intent, and it does not touch the safety-valve counters. It once read "Overrides `goal_mode`" two clauses above the paragraph saying `goal_mode` is untouched; a weak model acts on the first clause it can execute and makes the bare `saipen goal` resume illegal under this same section. **A tripped valve outranks the brake in one respect**: if the goal intent is set and `goal_waves`/`goal_tickets` are at or over MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4's caps, `saipen stop` MUST write the MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4 safety-valve `WAIT:`, never a `user brake` one -- stopping may pause a run, it may not disguise a tripped valve as a routine pause, which would hide the fact that continuing requires re-authorization. **This is a pause, not an exit**: the goal intent is left exactly as-is -- MAINTENANCE.md § 2.4's Exit list is exhaustive and `stop` is not on it -- and the safety-valve counters (`goal_waves`/`goal_tickets`) are left untouched by the stop itself too. How a *later resume* treats those counters depends on the valve, not on which command resumes: `cc` / `saipen continue` / bare `saipen` is the single reauthorization path -- the persisted `execution_intent` decides whether that is a goal resume (which resets a tripped valve's counters to `0` and continues the *same* objective, exactly as if the stop had never happened) or a converge resume. **Bare `saipen goal` never resumes anything**: it is the create/pivot command and, without text, the `Use: gg <objective text>` usage line -- it neither continues a paused run nor resets counters, so a stop-then-bare-goal-key cannot silently grant a fresh budget. The conditional reset lives with the resume command, not with the goal-create command: `reauthorize_valve` resets the counters to `0` only when they are at or over the caps, because a reset IS the re-authorization and an untripped valve has nothing to re-authorize. Against a tripped valve that reset is the whole point of `cc`; against an untripped one `cc` preserves the counters exactly as it would any resume.

- `saipen userperson` -- optional preference meta-control, OFF by default. With no `.saipen/USERPERSON.md` this command adds nothing to cold start: no warning, no boot failure, no placeholder, no onboarding, no cold-start cost. Invoked while absent it runs lightweight onboarding -- at most three broad questions (prefer two), or a free-form paste that bypasses the questions -- distilling direction and preferences, never biography, and writes the canonical `.saipen/USERPERSON.md` only after explicit user activation. USERPERSON means DEFAULT DIRECTION, not ORDER: precedence is current explicit request > project/task requirements > SAIPEN > verified evidence > USERPERSON, and it is used only where a valid implementation choice remains unspecified.
  - `saipen userperson show` -- print the current profile; read-only, never a write.
  - `saipen userperson add <text>` -- merge the given preferences into the profile. Merging is semantic: an addition already covered by an equivalent preference is a no-op, never an appended duplicate of history.
  - `saipen userperson remove <text>` -- remove the matching preference.
  - `saipen userperson reset` -- delete the profile; requires explicit destructive confirmation before acting (§ 1.1).
  Only explicit user input modifies USERPERSON. No passive profiling; no mining LOG/chat into permanent preferences. When a USERPERSON preference materially influenced a decision the current task did NOT already specify, report it once at completion as `USERPERSON alignment:`; when a relevant preference was deliberately overridden by stronger evidence or requirements, report one compact `USERPERSON deviation:`; never emit either trace when USERPERSON had no material effect, and never credit it for an explicit current instruction. SubSaipen handoffs project a relevant subset only (saiui: UI/design + workflow; saitranslate: localization/language; saiwiki: documentation/communication; saihunt: engineering rigor only where it affects the role) -- never the whole profile -- and record that a projection was supplied so Improve can audit the choice (T-574).
- `saipen improve [action]` -- META-CONTROL audit, NOT a phase. CORE owns
  routing; `saipen/IMPROVE.md` owns every action's lifecycle semantics. The
  complete public family is declared below and mechanically compared with
  IMPROVE.md and the CLI executor. `clean` is archive/retention only and never enters the CLEAN phase. No repeated-letter shortcut is assigned; reports
  live under `.saipen/improve/<cycle_id>/<seat_id>/`, never under the shared
  protocol install. Bare/`--new-seat` allocates a new independent seat;
  `--session <seat_id>` alone resumes one exact seat; `--role core|critic`
  selects the closed identity role while Core remains the only adjudicator.

`IMPROVE_ACTIONS = [bare, status, submit, complete, sweep, sweep-queue, verify, cycle-complete, abort, clean]`

**Any recognized phase-switching command (`clean`, `translate`, `markhunt`, `validate`, `prepare`, `plan`, `ship`, `hunt`) invoked while a ticket sits in `## DOING` MUST checkpoint that ticket first** (§ 1.5's normal LOG -> BOARD -> STATE, plus a refreshed `claim_time` if the ticket stays claimed) before switching phase. `saipen stop` already says this explicitly; the others inherit the same duty rather than each restating it, because the failure is identical either way -- switching phase out from under an in-flight ticket abandons it mid-edit, leaves an unrefreshed claim that goes stale in 15 minutes (§ 1.4), and strands whatever `kitchen/` work it had going. The ticket may legitimately stay `## DOING` across the switch (you're coming back to it) or be demoted to `## TODO` with a resumable `next_action` (you're not) -- what's forbidden is neither: leaving it claimed, unlogged, and unrecorded while the session goes and does something else.

**Repeated-letter shortcuts.** Identical keystrokes, no shift — a repeated letter cannot begin an English word, and a shortcut is the ENTIRE message rather than a prefix of one, so it is never mistaken for prose. **A shortcut is a command, never a greeting.** The ENTIRE-message rule exists so a bare key cannot be read as a prefix of prose, and the command rule exists so it cannot be read as prose at all: `qq` is not a hello, it is `saipen prepare saiwiki`. Receiving any declared shortcut (Latin or Cyrillic twin), the agent MUST execute the exact row it resolves to, or that row's exact no-op, and MUST NOT answer with an acknowledgment, a question, or any other chat. Reproduced on this repository at E-1801: a bare `qq` was answered as a greeting instead of executed as `saipen prepare saiwiki` -- the exact failure this sentence exists to name. **Length has no global meaning.** It resolves a letter's own pair: `ss`/`sss` are stop/status; `cc`/`ccc` are continue/continue-and-ship; `ee`/`eee` and `qq`/`qqq` are prepare/collect-and-ship. A doubled key can therefore pivot, scan, stop, report, plan, prepare, or test; a tripled key can be read-only or reach a remote. Cost and safety come only from the exact row, never from key count. `gg`, `hh`, and `cc` are explicit user routes; the rest are also assignments, not guessed initials. One key, one purpose: `gg` is the sole short route for a NEW goal, `cc` is continue/converge, and they never overlap. **A row's Notes describe what the destination actually does, including its bare form.** `cc`'s once claimed the bare key starts Goal Mode, which bare `saipen goal` is forbidden to do -- § 1.10 above requires it to write nothing and reply with the usage line at `execution_intent: normal`. On the most-used key the table promised an outcome the row cannot produce, which is the same class as the greeting misread one column over: the shortcut is a command, and a Notes column that describes a different command is the table lying about its own row. The table is authoritative and exhaustive: do not invent an undeclared repeated form from the pattern. **And answering a row from recall is the same failure as inventing a command, not a lesser one.** Read this table when a shortcut arrives; do not remember it. The rule above catches a key you know you do not know, which is the easy case -- the expensive one is a key you are sure about, where nothing prompts a lookup and the answer is fluent, specific and wrong. Observed: an agent asked `hh` produced a ten-row card in which every row was wrong and six of the command names it listed appear nowhere in this section, having first executed `cc` as a commit. Each row routes to the exact command chain shown, and `tools/validate.py` MUST reject both unknown commands and a valid-but-wrong destination.

| Shortcut | Routes to | Notes |
|----------|-----------|-------|
| `gg` | `saipen goal` | NEW GOAL ONLY: the pivot needs text, and `gg <objective>` is what sets a new one. Bare `gg` is never a continuation alias and never a resume -- it replies exactly `Use: gg <objective text>` and stops; straight after a plan command in the same message it starts the plan it just saw |
| `hh` | `saipen hunt` | Autonomous defect/improvement scan (MAINTENANCE.md § 2.1) |
| `cc` | `saipen continue` | CONTINUE CONTEXT / CONVERGE. Bare `cc` resumes `execution_intent: goal` if set, resumes convergence if set, and enters convergence from `normal` -- it never asks for an objective and never invents features. `cc <args>` is not a goal: it replies exactly `Use: gg <objective>` and stops |
| `ccc` | `saipen continue` with `converge_target: ship`, then `saipen ship`, then stages J-M | After checkpointing any current work, atomically set `execution_intent: converge` plus `converge_target: ship` (clear goal counters, preserve its real tickets), LOG the pre-SHIP source_head marker, converge through I, SHIP normally through every gate, then -- after the shipped revision -- sync and force-fresh EE and QQ against the shipped HEAD. Waives no review gate, destructive confirmation, first-publish confirmation, remote safety, or producer freshness rule |
| `ss` | `saipen stop` | The brake: checkpoint, write the digest, hand control back |
| `sss` | `saipen status` | Where the run stands, caveman-дед short |
| `dd` | `saipen plan` | Planning and adding both. Bare: autonomous 4-best-additions proposal. `dd <text>`: the user's own items, reworded and inserted at the front of `## TODO` |
| `aa` | `saipen markhunt` | Dry audit — records to BOARD, never fixes |
| `qq` | `saipen prepare saiwiki` | FORCE-FRESH regenerate and verify the complete maintained wiki as a new package bound to current `source_head + source_tree_fingerprint + role_revision`; spawn/adopt `saiwiki` internally if needed; no integration or push |
| `qqq` | `saipen collect saiwiki` then `saipen ship` | Ready-only wiki integration, gates, commit, and push; otherwise exact no-op: `Not ready: run qq first.` |
| `ee` | `saipen prepare saitranslate` | FORCE-FRESH regenerate and verify all real docs and in-app translation surfaces as a new package bound to current `source_head + source_tree_fingerprint + role_revision`; no integration or push |
| `eee` | `saipen collect saitranslate` then `saipen ship` | Ready-only translation integration, gates, commit, and push; otherwise exact no-op: `Not ready: run ee first.` **Never edit OUTBOX.md to force `status: ready` over incomplete coverage — the gate is the evidence, forging it is indistinguishable from the work being done** |
